:: Lost Lamb Verdict Widgets [widget]
<<widget 'lost-lamb-verdict'>>
  <<switch $LostLamb.scene>>
    <<case 'verdict-start'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          There have been other afternoons since the last one you remember clearly. Rain kept you indoors for one visit. On another, Sydney arrived with a scrape on <<nnpc_his 'Sydney'>> knee and insisted it did not hurt. You remember arguing about a game, then forgetting the argument before either of you apologised. The visits did not always go perfectly. You never needed them to.
          <br><br>
          Today the clock has gone past the time when the bell usually rings. You have moved your chair nearer the window. The gate is still shut. A person walking a dog pauses outside, and for a moment you are sure there is somebody behind them. There is only the dog, sniffing the same stone until the person pulls it away.
          <br><br>
          Downstairs, your parents ask whether you have finished putting your things away. You say yes. They tell you to find something to do. Neither mentions Sydney. You remember hearing that <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> might come today, although you cannot remember who said it. The words are perfectly clear. The mouth that made them will not stay in your memory.
          <br><br>
          You check the window catch. It is open. You could call through it if someone arrived. Outside, the garden path bends behind a hedge, and you keep watching the place where a head would appear first. At last you climb down. Your parents have stopped talking in the kitchen. A spoon rings once against a cup after the voices stop, much louder than it should. You decide to look at the gate yourself. Waiting upstairs might have made you miss something.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          距离最后那个记得清楚的下午，已经又有过几次来访。一次下雨，你们只能在屋里玩。另一次，悉尼膝盖擦破了，却坚持说不痛。你记得为一个游戏吵过嘴，还没等谁道歉，就忘了刚才在争什么。你们不是每一次都玩得很顺利。你也从没要求过这一点。
          <br><br>
          今天，钟已经走过门铃通常会响的时间。你把椅子挪得更靠近窗户，大门仍然关着。遛狗的人在外面停下，你一瞬间认定那人身后还有谁。那里只有一条狗，不停地嗅同一块石头，直到被牵走。
          <br><br>
          楼下，父母问你有没有收好东西。你说收好了。他们让你自己找点事情做，谁也没提悉尼。你记得听过<<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>>今天也许会来，却想不起是谁说的。那句话清清楚楚，说出它的嘴却留不在记忆里。
          <br><br>
          你检查窗扣，窗户开着。真有人来，可以从这里喊。花园小路绕过树篱，你始终盯着脑袋最先露出来的位置。最后还是爬下椅子。厨房里的父母已经不再说话。声音停了以后，勺子碰杯子响了一下，比刚才说话还响。你决定亲自去看看大门。一直在楼上等，也许反而漏掉了什么。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-coat'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          Sydney's hook is empty. You know it is <<nnpc_his 'Sydney'>> hook because you used to move your coat away from it. There are other hooks, but this is the one you cleared. A pale rectangle on the wood marks where somebody has taken down a little label. You cannot remember a label ever being there.
          <br><br>
          Your own coat has fallen. One sleeve reaches across the mat towards the door. You pick it up, and something in the lining makes a small dry crack. Not a broken bone. You tell yourself that before there is any reason to think about bones. It is the paper you left in the pocket, creased into a thick square. You do not open it yet.
          <br><br>
          The gate has been locked. You can see the key on its usual nail through the little window. Beyond the bars, children pass on the opposite pavement. None turns towards the house. You imagine Sydney being among them, trying not to look. Then you imagine <<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>> not being there at all. Both pictures hurt in different places.
          <br><br>
          From the kitchen, one of your parents calls your name. The voice sounds impatient, which is almost a relief. You know what to do with impatience. You can say you are coming and go in. You keep your coat against your chest for a moment longer. Beneath it, the hook you left free is only a hook. There is no hidden message written on it. You want to check anyway. When you turn to leave, the window catches the stairs behind you. Someone appears to be standing on the landing, though the stairs are empty when you look directly.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          悉尼的挂钩空着。你知道这是<<nnpc_his 'Sydney'>>的，因为以前会把自己的外套挪开。明明还有别的挂钩，你偏要给<<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>>留这一只。木头上有个浅色的小长方形，像刚揭掉一张标签。你却不记得那里贴过标签。
          <br><br>
          你的外套掉了，一只袖子横在地垫上，伸向门口。你捡起来，衬里发出干脆的一小声。不是骨头断了。还没有任何理由想到骨头，你已经这样告诉自己。只是口袋里的纸，被折成了厚厚的小方块。你暂时没有打开。
          <br><br>
          大门锁着。透过小窗，可以看到钥匙仍挂在惯常的钉子上。对面的人行道有几个孩子经过，谁也不看这所房子。你想象悉尼就在其中，故意不往这边看。随后又想象<<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>>根本没在那里。这两个念头，痛的地方不一样。
          <br><br>
          厨房里，父母喊了你的名字。声音不耐烦，反倒让你松了口气。你知道怎样应付不耐烦。答一句来了，再走进去就好。你仍把外套抱在胸前，多停了一会儿。留空的挂钩只是一只挂钩，下面没有写着秘密。你还是想检查。转身时，小窗映出身后的楼梯，看起来有人站在楼梯口。等你直接望过去，那里却是空的。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-table'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          There is bread on the table and a knife too large for it. Somebody has cut the crust in an uneven line, then put the knife far from your place. You tear off a corner. It tastes ordinary. You take another bite to make sure, and one of your parents tells you not to fill yourself before supper.
          <br><br>
          They are speaking about a message. It has not arrived, or it arrived and was not what somebody expected. You try to join the words into one account. Each time you think you have done it, another small word makes the account wrong. Sent. Returned. Again. The adults do not sound frightened. They sound tired of having the conversation where other people can hear.
          <br><br>
          A hand comes down beside your plate. You draw your elbow in before being asked. The hand rests there, thumb touching a crumb, and you notice a little blue mark near the nail. You have seen that hand pour milk, fasten a button, pull a splinter out of your finger. You would recognise it even if the rest of your parent were behind a door. You do not know why you think about that now.
          <br><br>
          Your parents look towards you at the same time. One asks whether you want more bread. The other has stopped halfway through a sentence. You want to ask about Sydney, but you want them to finish that sentence too. It feels as though you can have only one. Across the table, the unused chair has been pushed in so tightly that its back touches the edge. There is no plate in front of it. You remember setting a plate there. Perhaps that was a different day.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          桌上放着面包，还有一把对它来说太大的刀。面包皮切得参差不齐，刀被放到离你的位置很远的地方。你撕下一角，味道很普通。为了确认，又吃了一口，父母让你别在晚饭前把自己塞饱。
          <br><br>
          他们在谈一封消息。它还没来，也可能来了，却不是某个人想要的东西。你努力把那些词连成一件事。每次觉得已经连上，又有一个小词让整件事不对。寄出。退回。又一次。大人听起来并不害怕，更像厌烦了在别人能听见的地方讨论这些。
          <br><br>
          一只手落在你的盘子旁。还没被提醒，你就收回胳膊肘。手停在那里，拇指碰着一粒面包屑，指甲边有一点蓝色。你见过这只手倒牛奶、扣扣子，把刺从你的手指里挑出来。即使父母的其余部分藏在门后，你也能认出它。你不知道为什么现在想到这一点。
          <br><br>
          父母同时看向你。一位问还要不要面包，另一位停在说到一半的句子里。你想问悉尼，也想让他们把那句话讲完。感觉只能选一样。对面的空椅子推得很紧，椅背碰到了桌沿，前面没放盘子。你记得自己在那里摆过一只盘子。也许是另一天。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-words'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-asked-visit')>>"Not today," one of your parents says. You ask which day instead. The knife is moved farther away. "You heard me." You did hear. You have heard that kind of answer before. It makes your face hot.<<else>>You keep your eyes on the bread. When the adults begin speaking again, you let your chewing become slower. Somebody notices and tells you to swallow. You swallow before you are ready and spend the next moment trying not to cough.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          "It would be easier if—" The sentence ends with a chair moving across the floor. Your parent stands to take a cup to the sink, and running water covers what comes next. You have the beginning. It would be easier if. You imagine three different endings, and all three make you wish you had not started listening.
          <br><br>
          A name is spoken behind the water. For a moment it is yours. Then it has too many syllables, or too few. Your parent turns the tap off, shakes water from the cup, and puts it down without drying it. The clear ring it leaves spreads through a sheet lying on the counter. Nobody moves the paper until the ring has reached the writing.
          <br><br>
          They tell you to go upstairs. The command is gentle. That is worse than impatience, because you cannot tell what would happen if you refused. You take your coat with you, although it is warm indoors. On the stairs, the unfinished sentence begins again in your head. It would be easier if. Behind you, the kitchen door closes before anybody says the rest. You stand still long enough to hear the latch settle. There is another door above the kitchen, the one between the landing and the passage beside the laboratory. From there you might hear a whole sentence, and you might not need to ask anything.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-asked-visit')>>“今天不来。”父母说。你问那是哪一天，刀又被挪远了一点。“你已经听见了。”你当然听见了。这种回答你听过，它让你脸发热。<<else>>你盯着面包。大人又开始说话，你让咀嚼慢下来。有人发现，叫你咽下去。你没准备好就咽了，接下来只能拼命忍住咳嗽。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          “要是……就好办了。”一句话停在椅子擦过地板的声音里。父母起身把杯子拿到水槽边，流水盖住了后半句。你只有开头。要是。你替它想了三种结尾，三种都让你后悔刚才去听。
          <br><br>
          水声后说出一个名字。一瞬间是你的，接着却又多了几个音节，或少了几个。父母关上龙头，甩掉杯里的水，没有擦干就放下。杯子留下的水圈在台面上的纸里扩散，碰到字迹以前，谁也没有挪动它。
          <br><br>
          他们让你上楼。语气很温和，比不耐烦更难受，因为你听不出拒绝会怎样。屋里很暖，你还是带着外套。走到楼梯上，那句没说完的话又在脑子里开始。要是。身后的厨房门关上了，没人说出剩下的部分。你站住，等门闩落稳。厨房上面还有一道门，通往实验室旁边的过道。到那里，也许能听见一个完整的句子，不用再去问。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-landing-entry'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The landing is warmer than the stairs. A narrow strip of light lies under the connecting door. You remember being allowed through there once with a stack of folded towels. Somebody carried the top half of the stack because you could not see over it. That seems a very long time ago, though the towels have not become any smaller.
          <br><br>
          You put your coat on the banister. From below, it might look like someone standing here. You take it off again. There are places where you could sit without being seen: beside the cold outer wall, behind the door when it opens, or on the little step where the floor drops into the other passage. Each has a reason for being uncomfortable. You try to decide which discomfort will make the least noise.
          <br><br>
          Beyond the door, your parents are speaking to somebody. The other voice is quieter than theirs. It does not sound strange until you try to remember what a stranger should sound like. You hear a chair creak beneath its weight. That helps. There is a person sitting in a chair. Whatever they are saying, it is being said by people who need somewhere to sit.
          <br><br>
          On the mat below are shoes brought in from the garden. You could carry them to the cupboard. That would give you a reason to pass the door. Sitting on the step would be quieter. Putting your ear near the handle would let you hear more. You cannot do everything without staying here too long. Your parent calls something downstairs, and a reply comes from the laboratory side. You glance between the two places. For a moment you have heard the same voice from both. Then a laugh ends the conversation, and you no longer know which voice began it.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          楼梯口比台阶暖，一条窄窄的光从连通门下透出来。你记得有一次能从这里过去，手里抱着叠好的毛巾。有人替你拿走上面的一半，因为你已经看不见前面。好像很久以前的事了，毛巾却没有变小。
          <br><br>
          你把外套搭在栏杆上。从楼下看，也许像有人站在这里，于是又拿下来。有几个不容易被看见的位置。冰冷的外墙边，门打开后被遮住的地方，还有通往另一条过道的小台阶。每处都有不舒服的理由。你得判断哪一种不舒服最不容易弄出声音。
          <br><br>
          门后，父母正和某个人说话。另一个声音比他们轻。起先并不奇怪，直到你试图想起陌生人应该怎样说话。你听见椅子承受重量时吱了一声，这让你好受一点。那里有个人，坐在椅子里。无论他们讨论什么，都还是由需要椅子的人说出来的。
          <br><br>
          楼下的地垫上有从花园穿回来的鞋。你可以拿去柜子，经过门口就有理由。坐在台阶上更安静，把耳朵凑到把手附近却能听得更多。不能全做，待得太久会被发现。父母往楼下喊了一句，实验室方向传来回答。你看向两边，刚才像是从两个地方听见了同一个声音。接着一阵笑结束对话，你再也分不出哪个声音先开口。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-landing'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-landing-return')>>You are back beside the banister. The light beneath the door has grown thinner. You know where the noisy boards are now, but knowing makes you watch your feet instead of the room. <<if Time.hour lt 17 or (Time.hour is 17 and Time.minute lt 30)>>The adults are still near the connecting door. You could risk that position before half past five, or keep listening from the step.<<else>>The voices have moved away from the connecting door. Putting your ear there would reveal nothing now. You can still hear through the floor by the step, or find an ordinary reason to pass with the shoes.<</if>> <span class='blue'>Staying longer can make you easier to notice. Once you leave this landing, this chance will be gone.</span><<else>>The door has a brass plate low enough for you to see your knees in it. They look as though they belong to somebody waiting behind you. You move one foot. The reflected foot moves too, after you have almost decided it will not.
          <br><br>
          You do not need to open the door. You only need to be close when the adults finish what they have started saying. You imagine going back to your room with the whole sentence, setting it down like a cup that will not spill, and not having to guess about Sydney anymore. Then somebody inside mentions a promise. You lean forward before deciding to.
          <br><br>
          There are three ways to remain nearby. The stone step is out of the direct light. The connecting door is thin enough to hear through, though touching it might move the handle. The shoes downstairs need putting away, and carrying them would give you something ordinary to explain. You cannot tell which explanation will sound ordinary when spoken aloud. Explanations change when an adult is looking directly at you.
          <br><br>
          A chair leg scrapes. You hear your name and then the word later. They might be talking about you. They might only be saying that supper can wait until later. The difference is everything, and you have missed the words between. <span class='blue'>The conversation will move away from the thin door at half past five. Choose that position first if you want to hear there. Moving quietly reduces the chance of being noticed.</span><</if>>
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-landing-return')>>你回到栏杆边。门下的光更窄了。现在知道哪些木板容易响，却因此只顾看脚，不敢看屋子。<<if Time.hour lt 17 or (Time.hour is 17 and Time.minute lt 30)>>大人仍在连通门附近。你可以在五点半前去门边冒一次险，也可以继续从台阶边听。<<else>>声音已经离开连通门。现在把耳朵贴过去，也听不出什么了。台阶边仍能隔着地板听，拿鞋经过也仍有普通的理由。<</if>><span class='blue'>待得越久，越容易引起注意。离开楼梯口后，就不能再利用这一次机会。</span><<else>>门上有一块很低的黄铜护板，映着你的膝盖，像属于站在身后等候的另一个人。你动一只脚。映出的脚也动了，就在你差点认定它不会跟着动的时候。
          <br><br>
          不需要开门，只需要在大人把话说完时离得够近。你想象带着完整的句子回到房间，像放下一杯不会洒出来的水，从此不必再猜悉尼为什么没来。里面有人提到一个约定，还没决定，你已经往前倾了。
          <br><br>
          有三种办法留在附近。石台阶不在光里，连通门薄得能听见声音，可碰到门，也许把手会动。楼下的鞋需要收好，拿着鞋，就有普通的事情可以解释。你不知道哪一种解释说出来还会普通。被大人直接盯着时，解释总会变样。
          <br><br>
          椅腿擦过地板。你听见自己的名字，又听见以后。他们也许在说你，也许只说晚饭可以以后再吃。两种意思差得太远，中间的词却漏掉了。<span class='blue'>五点半以后，谈话就会移离薄门。想从门边听，得先选那个位置。动作安静些，可以少引起注意。</span><</if>>
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-step'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The stone takes the warmth out of your legs through your shorts. You put your coat beneath you. One trainer is touching the board above the step, and you draw it back until there is nothing between your sole and the cold floor. You have never sat so carefully in your own house.
          <br><br>
          At first you hear only the clock. It makes a soft scraping noise before each click. Once you notice it, you cannot stop hearing it. You try counting the clicks, because counting is something you can do without moving, and lose count whenever one of the voices rises. Someone says that a child remembers more than people think. You look down at your hands as though they have been caught doing something.
          <br><br>
          "Not that part." Your parent says it quickly. The quiet voice replies, but you miss the reply. You have heard the objection clearly and none of what was objected to. It would be easy to give it a shape. A secret. A mistake. A bad thing done deliberately. Each would fit into the little space left by the missing words. You press your tongue against a loose edge of skin inside your cheek until it hurts.
          <br><br>
          Something drops near the other side of the door. Not something heavy. A button, perhaps, or the end of a pencil. It rolls towards you. You see a dark bead stop in the strip of light, and realise you do not know which way the floor slopes. The adults stop speaking. You keep your eyes on the bead, waiting for a finger to pick it up. A shadow covers it instead. When the shadow moves, the bead is gone. Your foot has begun to tingle. You need to move before standing up will make you stumble.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          石头隔着短裤，把腿上的热气吸走。你把外套垫在下面，一只运动鞋碰到了上方的木板，又慢慢收回，直到鞋底与冰冷地面之间不再隔着别的东西。在自己家里，你从没坐得这么小心。
          <br><br>
          最先只听见钟。每次滴答前，它会轻轻刮一下。发现以后，就再也忽略不了。你试着数，因为数数不用动，可一有人提高声音，就会忘记数到哪里。有人说，孩子记得比人们以为的多。你低头看自己的手，像它们刚被抓住做了坏事。
          <br><br>
          “不是那一部分。”父母很快地说。轻声的来客回答了，你却没听见。只听清反对，没有听见被反对的内容。很容易替它添出形状。秘密。错误。故意做的坏事。每一种都放得进漏掉词句留下的空缺。你用舌头抵着脸颊内一小块翘起的皮，直到开始痛。
          <br><br>
          门那边掉了东西，不重，也许是纽扣或笔的一端。它朝你滚来。一颗黑珠子停在光里，你才发现自己不知道地板往哪边斜。大人停止说话。你盯着珠子，等一根手指把它捡起，却只有影子盖过来。影子挪开，珠子就不见了。你的脚已经开始发麻，得赶在起身会摔倒之前换个位置。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-listening-door'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You put one hand on the wall rather than the door. Paint flakes under your thumbnail. You stop picking at it. The little movements you make without thinking are the ones that might give you away. Your ear is close enough to feel the cold of the metal plate, but you do not let it touch.
          <br><br>
          Your parent is talking about a visit that cannot happen again. The word again is clear. The rest comes through as separate sounds, blurred wherever a chair or a cup moves. You think of Sydney at the gate and lean closer. On the other side, somebody takes a long breath. It seems to reach your ear through the keyhole, warmer than the room you are standing in.
          <br><br>
          "We agreed." You know those words. Adults use them when they want a conversation to stop. You use them too, when someone changes the rules of a game after losing. The voice behind the door says them without sounding angry. That makes you wonder whether agreement can be worse than an argument. There is a short laugh afterwards. You cannot tell who laughs. You have heard both your parents laugh often enough. This ought to be easy.
          <br><br>
          The handle shifts. You pull back, but your sleeve has caught on the little ridge beneath it. For a moment the cloth holds you beside the door. You have enough time to imagine the door opening with you still attached, being pulled into the bright room like a caught fish. It does not open. You slide the sleeve free. A dark line appears across your wrist where the fabric pressed. The voices resume. You are no longer sure whether they know you are here and have decided not to show it yet.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你把一只手按在墙上，没有碰门。指甲下剥落了一点漆，你停止抠它。那些不经意的小动作，最可能把你暴露。耳朵离金属护板很近，能感觉到它的凉，却还没有贴上去。
          <br><br>
          父母在谈一次不能再发生的来访。又一次，听得很清楚。其余都是断开的声音，每遇到杯子或椅子挪动，就被弄糊。你想到站在大门口的悉尼，凑得更近。门那边，有人长长吸了口气，像穿过锁孔直达耳边，比你站着的房间还暖。
          <br><br>
          “说好了的。”你认得这句话。大人想让谈话停止时会用，别人输了游戏就改规则的时候，你也会这样说。门后的声音说起来一点不生气，让你怀疑，达成一致是不是有时比争吵更坏。接着有人短短地笑，你分不出是谁。父母的笑你听过那么多次，这明明该很容易。
          <br><br>
          把手动了。你往后退，袖子却挂在下面的小凸起上，有一瞬间被布料拴在门边。足够想象门打开时，自己还挂在那里，像一条钓上的鱼，被拖进明亮的屋子。门没有开。你把袖子抽出来，手腕留下被勒出的深色细线。谈话继续了。你已不能确定，他们是不是知道你在这里，只决定暂时不揭穿。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-shoes'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You go down for the shoes. There is dry soil caught in the grooves, and a leaf stuck to the heel. You rub the leaf off with your thumb. Your parent would tell you not to carry dirty things against your shirt. You hold the shoes out in front of you and become very aware of how far your arms reach.
          <br><br>
          Halfway up, you pass the window. The shoes are reflected beside your head, as though two people stand behind you with their feet at shoulder height. You turn them sole to sole. The reflection becomes a bundle of dark leather. You do not like either arrangement, and you are angry with yourself for needing to change it.
          <br><br>
          At the cupboard, one of your parents sees you. You are asked what you are doing. You lift the shoes, and the question ends before you answer. A small ordinary task has made you almost invisible. You take longer than you need to put them away. Behind you, the quiet visitor says something about a child being kept out of it. Your parent replies that this has already been done. You wonder how anybody can be sure a child is out of something when nobody has told the child where its edge is.
          <br><br>
          There is a pair of boots at the back of the cupboard. You have never worn them. Their toes face the wall. You turn one to make room, and a little heap of grit falls out of the opening. Nobody tells you to stop. That makes the task feel safer. You shut the cupboard softly, with one finger keeping the latch from clicking until the last moment. You can stand here a little longer if you still look as though you are finishing. Eventually your empty hands will give you away.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你下楼拿鞋。鞋纹里卡着干土，后跟粘着叶子，用拇指擦下来。父母会说，脏东西不能贴在衣服上拿。你把鞋举在身前，突然十分在意自己的胳膊究竟能伸多远。
          <br><br>
          走到一半，经过窗户，鞋被映在你的头旁，像身后站着两个人，脚却到了肩膀那么高。你把鞋底合在一起，倒影变成一团黑皮革。两种摆法都不喜欢，也气自己居然非得去改。
          <br><br>
          到柜子前，父母看见你，问在做什么。你抬起鞋，还没回答，问题就结束了。一件普通的小事，让你几乎隐了形。收鞋时故意多花了一会儿。背后，轻声的来客说到应该让孩子置身事外，父母说，这已经做到了。没人告诉孩子一件事的边界在哪里，又怎么能确定孩子是在外面呢。
          <br><br>
          柜子深处有一双靴子，你从没穿过，鞋尖朝着墙。你转动一只，好腾出位置，靴筒口落出一小堆砂土。没人叫你别动，于是这件事更安全了。关柜子时，一根手指压着闩，直到最后才松开，尽量不让它响。只要看起来还没收完，就能多站一会儿。最终，空空的两只手还是会出卖你。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-overheard'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          Nobody opens the door. You hear the quiet visitor say, "Before anyone changes their mind." Your parent answers, "Not in front of—" A second voice says your name. The three pieces arrive separately, like things dropped through a hole in a fence. You can put them in order. You cannot be certain they belong together.
          <br><br>
          There is a sound like paper being pulled from beneath a cup. Somebody asks whether there is enough time. The reply is so quick that you take it for yes, until the quiet visitor says, "Then why ask?" You almost step closer again. You have learnt something, and learning it has made the rest harder to leave alone.
          <br><br>
          You look at the light beneath the door. Two shadows lie across it. One moves when your parent speaks. The other does not move when the visitor speaks, but it is much farther from the light. You cannot count the people from their shadows. You try anyway. Perhaps the person whose shadow does not move is the one who has not decided. Perhaps you are making another sentence from pieces that never belonged together.
          <br><br>
          Then your parent says that a visit must stop. It is not a question. Nobody says Sydney's name. You know whose visit you want them to mean and whose you want them not to mean. That wish slides into the gap so smoothly that you nearly mistake it for something you have heard. You press your forehead to the cool wall. A chair shifts inside. The shadows merge, and you step back before the door can open. You can still get away without an explanation. You also know you will keep listening to the missing words long after leaving the landing.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          没人开门。轻声的来客说：“趁还没人改主意。”父母回答：“别当着……”另一个声音说出你的名字。三截话分开到来，像从栅栏洞里扔进来的东西。可以排列，却不能确定原本连在一起。
          <br><br>
          有纸从杯子下面抽出的声音。有人问时间够不够，回答太快，你先以为是够，随后来客说：“那为什么还问？”你差点又往前挪。听见了一点，反而更难放下其余部分。
          <br><br>
          你看门下的光。两道影子压在上面，一道在父母说话时移动。来客说话时另一道没动，却也可能离光太远。不能靠影子数屋里的人，你还是试了。也许不动的那一道属于尚未决定的人。也许又在把原本不相连的碎片拼成句子。
          <br><br>
          随后父母说，一次来访必须停止，不是在提问。谁也没说悉尼的名字。你知道自己希望是哪个人的来访，也希望不是哪个人的。那个愿望滑进空缺，贴合得太好，差点被当成亲耳听见的东西。你把额头抵在凉墙上。里面椅子挪动，两道影子合起来。赶在门打开前，你退开。仍来得及不用解释就离开，可你知道，离开楼梯口以后，也会一直听着那些没听见的词。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-noticed'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The door opens towards you. You have time to move your foot, not the rest of yourself. The edge stops against your knee. Your parent looks down through the gap and does not speak. You realise you are holding your coat like something stolen, though it has always been yours.
          <br><br>
          "How long?" You know what is being asked. You could say the time, but you do not know it. You could say you have just come upstairs, which would be almost true if the shoes had brought you here. The visitor is somewhere behind your parent's shoulder. You can see a chair arm and a cuff resting on it. Nothing else. The cuff is dark, or the room behind it is dark. You would like to choose which before answering.
          <br><br>
          Your parent moves the door away from your knee. That small consideration makes you feel worse. If you were being shouted at, you could get angry. You are being looked at instead. The quiet visitor says, "Children hear names." Your parent says, "I know." The words are spoken over your head, and you wish you were tall enough to get in their way.
          <br><br>
          You have two explanations. There are cups downstairs that need carrying, and you really did hear your name. The first gives you somewhere to put your hands. The second would require somebody to say what your name was doing in the conversation. You do not know which is more dangerous. <span class='red'>You have been noticed.</span> <span class='blue'>An ordinary task can lower that attention. Admitting what you heard may expose a different part of the conversation.</span> Your knee aches where the door touched it. You look at the ache instead of the cuff, and your parent asks the question again.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          门朝你打开。只来得及挪脚，身体没来得及。门沿抵住膝盖，父母从缝里往下看，一句话也不说。你发现自己抱着外套，像抱着偷来的东西，尽管它一直都是你的。
          <br><br>
          “多久了？”你知道问的是什么。可以报个时间，却根本不知道。可以说刚刚上楼，要是鞋把你带到这里，这句话就几乎是真的。来客在父母肩后某处，你只看见扶手，还有搁在上面的袖口。袖口很黑，也可能只是后面的屋子黑。你希望先弄清哪一种，再回答。
          <br><br>
          父母把门挪离你的膝盖。这一点照顾让你更难受。要是挨骂，还能生气，现在却只是被看着。轻声的来客说：“孩子会听见名字。”父母说：“我知道。”两句话从头顶越过，你希望自己够高，能够挡住它们。
          <br><br>
          有两种解释。楼下确实有杯子需要搬，你也确实听见了自己的名字。前一种让手上有事做，后一种却会要求有人说清，你的名字为何出现在谈话里。不知道哪一种更危险。<span class='red'>你已经引起注意。</span><span class='blue'>普通的事情可以让他们少盯着你，承认听见的话，也可能让另一部分谈话露出来。</span>门碰过的膝盖还在痛。你盯着那点痛，不再看袖口，父母又把问题问了一遍。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-watched'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-admitted')>>"I heard my name." Once you have said it, you cannot make yourself smaller than the words. Your parent says your name again, softly, as though showing you that it can be used for an ordinary reason. You wait for the rest. There is no rest.<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-cup-excuse')>>You say something about cups. Your parent asks which cups. You point downstairs, and the pointing gives you a moment to think. "The ones on the table." There must be cups on the table. You have seen them all your life.<<else>>You reach the bottom of the stairs before the connecting door opens. You keep walking. Your parent sees you from above and asks whether you are cold, because you are carrying your coat indoors. You say a little. It becomes easier to believe once you have said it.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          You are told to sit in the kitchen. Not your room, where you wanted to go. A chair is brought out beside the table. From there you can see the garden door and the passage, and both can see you. Your parent gives you a cloth and a cup that is already dry. You dry it again. Working is easier than keeping your hands still.
          <br><br>
          The visitor passes the kitchen doorway. You look up too late for a face. There is a long shadow, then a hand against the frame, then neither. The hand had the wrong position for a person walking in that direction. You try the movement with your own hand below the table. It is possible, but awkward. You have made an ordinary explanation and still do not feel better.
          <br><br>
          Your parent touches the top of your head, briefly. You want the hand to stay. You also want to lean away from it, because the adults were saying your name when you were not supposed to be there. The hand leaves before you decide. The cup shines in your lap. <span class='blue'>Sitting in view gives the adults less reason to keep checking where you are.</span> If you heard clearly, you can still compare the voices with the places set at supper. If you were caught, carrying the cups will give you another ordinary way into the room.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-admitted')>>“我听见自己的名字。”说出口，就再也不能把自己缩得比这句话小。父母轻轻又叫你一次，像证明名字也能出于普通理由使用。你等后半句，没有后半句。<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-cup-excuse')>>你说到杯子。父母问哪些杯子。你指楼下，指过去的动作多给了一点思考时间。“桌上那些。”桌上总该有杯子，从小到大，你都在那里看见。<<else>>连通门打开以前，你已经下完楼梯，继续往前走。父母从上面看见你，问是不是冷，因为屋里还抱着外套。你说有一点。说出口，反倒更容易相信了。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          他们让你坐厨房，不是你想去的房间。一只椅子挪到桌边，从那里能看见花园门和过道，两个方向也都能看见你。父母给你布，还有一只已经干了的杯子。你又擦一遍。手上有事，比一动不动容易。
          <br><br>
          来客经过厨房门口。你抬头太晚，没看见脸。先是一条长影子，接着一只按在门框上的手，再接着都没有了。朝那个方向走，手的位置却不对。你在桌下用自己的手模仿，做得到，只是很别扭。找到了普通的解释，还是没有好受。
          <br><br>
          父母短暂摸了下你的头。希望那只手多留一会儿，又想躲开，因为刚才不许你在场时，大人却在说你的名字。还没决定，手就离开了。杯子在膝上发亮。<span class='blue'>坐在他们能看见的地方，他们就不必反复确认你去了哪里。</span>要是听得清楚，晚饭时仍能把声音与座位对上。要是被抓住，端杯子也能让你有普通的理由再次进去。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-dinner'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          Supper begins before the visitor comes in. There is a place for them, with the knife turned the wrong way round. You straighten it. Your parent immediately turns it back. Neither of you mentions what the other has done. You look at your own knife to be certain the rule has not changed for everybody.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-carrying-cups')>>You carry the cups in one at a time. The small job lets you pass behind the empty chair twice. There is nobody hiding there, and no coat on its back. You are relieved by something you would not have admitted checking.<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-matched-voices')>>You listen when your parents speak and look at the empty chair when the quieter voice answers from the passage. For a moment the voices line up with places. Then your parent answers a question you have not heard anybody ask.<<else>>You sit where you can see the doorway. Your parent asks you to move your chair in. You move it only a little, hoping that will be enough. The doorway remains visible between an elbow and the back of the empty chair.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          Food is brought in. Somebody has made your favourite part a little differently, and you almost complain. The complaint dies when you notice the adults watching whether you eat. You try a mouthful. It is good. That feels unfair. You want something on the table to tell you whether this is a good evening or a bad one, and food does not know.
          <br><br>
          At the doorway, the visitor stops. You see a shoulder first. It remains a shoulder even when the rest of the person should have come into view. Your parent lifts the serving dish and blocks the doorway. When the dish comes down, the visitor is sitting. You have missed the moment of entering. The empty place is full, but you cannot quite remember what filled it. You could ask a name. You could watch the hands instead. Both choices feel rude, and neither feels as rude as the adults pretending nothing has happened.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          来客进屋前，晚饭已经开始。给对方留了位置，刀的方向却反了。你摆正，父母立刻又转回去。谁都不提对方刚做的事。你看看自己的刀，确认是不是所有人的规则都变了。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-carrying-cups')>>你一只只端杯子，借这件小事从空椅子后面经过两次。那里没人藏着，椅背也没搭外套。你松了口气，又不肯承认自己刚才确实检查过。<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-matched-voices')>>父母说话时，你听着。轻一些的声音从过道回答，就看空椅子。一瞬间，声音与位置对上了。随后父母回答了一个你没听见有人问的问题。<<else>>你坐在能看见门口的地方。父母叫你把椅子往里挪，你只挪一点，希望这样就够。门口仍露在胳膊肘与空椅子背之间。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          食物端上桌。你最喜欢的部分做得和平时略有不同，差点抱怨。发现大人在看你吃不吃，那句话就没了。你尝了一口，很好吃，反而不公平。希望桌上有什么能告诉自己，这是一个好晚上还是坏晚上。食物却不知道。
          <br><br>
          来客停在门口，先露出肩膀。其余部分该出现时，却还只是一只肩膀。父母举起盛食物的盘子，挡住了门口。盘子落下，来客已经坐着。你漏掉了进屋的那一刻。空位置有人了，却记不牢究竟是谁填上它。可以问名字，也可以看手，两样都很失礼，却都不如大人假装什么也没发生那么失礼。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-visitor'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The visitor's cuff has not picked up a crumb. Below it, a narrow band of silver catches the candlelight. You lean sideways to see the face. Your parent lifts the jug, and by the time it comes down you have forgotten what you were looking for. The visitor's cup is still empty.
          <br><br>
          "You don't want anyone else getting hurt, do you?" You shake your head. "Good. Then you must be careful who you tell." Your parent sets the jug down hard enough to spill a little water. The visitor lays two fingers in the spill. The water draws back from the silver.
          <br><br>
          You ask whether that means Sydney can come again. The visitor turns the empty cup a quarter of the way round. "Children are safest at home." That still does not tell you whose home. You look at your parent. They have begun cutting the same piece of bread into smaller and smaller pieces.
          <br><br>
          At last the visitor lifts the cup and drinks. There was nothing in it. You watch the throat move, then look away before anyone notices. A quiet voice asks what else you would like to know. It sounds so kind that you nearly tell it everything.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          来客的袖口没有沾上一点面包屑。下面露出窄窄一线银色，映着烛光。你侧过身，想看清脸。父母举起水壶，再放下时，你已经忘了刚才想看什么。来客的杯子仍是空的。
          <br><br>
          “你不想再有人受伤，对吧？”你摇头。“很好。那就得小心，别随便向人说。”父母把水壶放得太重，洒出一点水。来客把两根手指放在水里，水从那一线银色那里退开。
          <br><br>
          你问，那悉尼还能来吗？来客把空杯转了四分之一圈。“孩子待在家里才安全。”仍然没说，是谁的家。你去看父母。他们把同一小片面包切得越来越碎。
          <br><br>
          来客终于举杯喝了一口。杯子里本来什么都没有。你看见喉咙动了，赶在别人发现前移开眼睛。轻柔的声音问，还想知道什么？听起来那么亲切，你差点把什么都说出去。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-question'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-house-question')>>"Which house?" you ask. The visitor points at the floor. "Here." You ask whether they have been upstairs. Your parent catches the visitor's sleeve, then lets go before the fingers have closed.<<else>>"Why can't Sydney come?" You have asked it before. This time the visitor lets you finish. Your parent looks towards the stairs.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          "There are things outside that listen at doors." The visitor does not raise the voice. "They only need one careless word." A fingernail touches the table. Somewhere behind you, a latch answers it. You look round. The kitchen door was open a moment ago.
          <br><br>
          Your parent says that nobody told them. The visitor's fingers stop moving. "You have been told now." You wait for the rest, but your parent presses the wet cloth flat against the spill and will not look up. You know how to wipe a table. You do not know how to help with this.
          <br><br>
          "Bed," the other parent says. You protest that it is early. The visitor turns the empty cup again, towards your place this time. "We can speak when you've thought about it." You leave without asking what you are meant to think about. As you climb, the kitchen latch lifts and drops behind you. Nobody has followed you out.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-house-question')>>“哪所房子？”你问。来客指了指地板。“这里。”你问有没有去过楼上。父母抓了一下来客的袖子，手指还没合拢就松开了。<<else>>“悉尼为什么不能来？”问过很多次。这次来客让你把话说完。父母看向楼梯。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          “外面有东西，会趴在门上听。”来客没有提高声音。“只要一个说漏嘴的词。”指甲碰到桌面，身后某处，门闩跟着响了一下。你回头，厨房门刚才还开着。
          <br><br>
          父母说，没有人告诉过他们。来客的手指停住了。“现在告诉你们了。”你等后面的话，可父母只把湿布平平地压在水迹上，不肯抬头。你知道怎么擦桌子，不知道怎么帮这种忙。
          <br><br>
          另一位父母说，该睡了。你抗议还很早。来客又转了转空杯，这次朝向你的座位。“等你想好了，我们再谈。”你没有问究竟该想什么，便离开了。上楼时，厨房门闩在身后抬起，又落下。没人跟着出来。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-night-call'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          There are several evenings between the table and this bed, but you cannot set them in a row. In one, you wash a cup. In another, your parents are excited about something and send you upstairs early. You turn onto your side and try to remember whether you washed the cup before or after they called.
          <br><br>
          Glass breaks below. Not a dropped cup. The sound is much larger, followed by many little falls, as though the broken thing has not finished breaking. You pull the covers over your face. Your shirt sticks against your back. Somebody is moving downstairs, quickly enough to knock furniture against the walls. You hear a voice, but not words. Another voice joins it or the first changes while taking a breath.
          <br><br>
          You try to remember who was sitting at supper. The clean cuff. A spoon. Your parent's hand beside the plate. The pieces come up without their owners. For a moment you see the visitor sitting on the end of your bed, holding the cup upright while glass falls from the ceiling around it. You open your eyes beneath the covers. There is only cloth against your nose. You never looked towards the end of the bed. You do not know where the picture came from.
          <br><br>
          A noise rises through the floor, too rough to be speech. You want your parents. You also want to be sure your parents are not what is making the noise. There is no way to ask one without risking the other. The handle of your door remains still. That should mean nobody is coming in. It might also mean nobody is coming to you. <span class='red'>The glass below keeps breaking. The voices do not answer you in words.</span> You put your hands over your ears, then take them away again. Not hearing would leave you alone with what you have already imagined.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          餐桌与这张床之间，应该还隔着几个晚上，却排不出先后。一次，你洗了一只杯子。另一次，父母为某件事兴奋，早早把你送上楼。你翻过身，努力回想，洗杯子是在他们喊你以前，还是以后。
          <br><br>
          楼下有玻璃碎了，不是掉了一只杯子。声音大得多，随后是许多小小的坠落，好像碎掉的东西还没碎完。你把被子拉到脸上，衣服粘着背。有人在楼下走得很急，把家具撞到墙上。听见一个声音，却没有词。另一个声音加入，也可能只是前一个在换气时变了。
          <br><br>
          你试着想起晚饭时谁坐在哪里。干净袖口。勺子。父母放在盘边的手。碎片浮上来，却不带主人。一瞬间，看见来客坐在床尾，把杯子扶正，四周天花板不断落下玻璃。你在被子下睁眼，鼻子前只有布。根本没朝床尾看过，不知道画面从哪来。
          <br><br>
          一个声音穿过地板，粗哑得不像能说话。你想要父母，又想确认那声音不是父母。两种愿望，无法问一个而不冒另一个的险。门把手始终没动，这该意味着没人进来，也可能意味着没人会来找你。<span class='red'>楼下仍有玻璃碎裂，声音没有用词句回答你。</span>你捂住耳朵，又松开。不听，只会让你与那些已经想象出来的东西单独待着。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-cup'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-called-parents')>>You called for your parents in the night, and nobody came. The house has not changed its mind by morning.<<else>>Morning arrives without anyone calling you.<</if>> You have fallen asleep in your clothes, one trainer pressed against the other. When you straighten your legs, the laces have caught together. You nearly cry before finding the knot. You pull it apart with both hands.
          <br><br>
          Downstairs, light comes through a window with part of the glass missing. The curtain lifts through the empty place. You had not imagined it breaking. You touch the wall below the window, where damp air has cooled the plaster, and take your hand away when something sharp catches your sleeve. A little triangle is lodged in the fabric. You leave it there until you can see which way to pull it out.
          <br><br>
          There is nobody in the kitchen. The visitor's place has been cleared. Your own plate is still on the table, with the food you pushed towards the middle. You do not know whether that means this is the next morning or whether several evenings have been squeezed into it. A cup stands by the sink. You turn it over to check whether it has been washed. The inside is clean. The handle is wet.
          <br><br>
          You fill it with water because water is something you know how to carry. If your parents are ill, they might need it. If they are angry, bringing it might make the first thing you say easier. The cup feels too hot in your fingers even though you used the cold tap. You set it down, wait, and lift it again. It is cold now. You cannot decide which feeling was mistaken. You take a cloth beneath it, not because you expect it to spill, but because the cloth gives your shaking fingers somewhere to hide.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-called-parents')>>夜里你喊过父母，没有人来。到了早晨，房子也没有改主意。<<else>>早晨到了，却没有人来喊你。<</if>>你穿着衣服睡着，两只运动鞋紧贴在一起。伸腿时，鞋带缠住了。你差点哭出来，才看见打结的地方，赶快用两只手解开。
          <br><br>
          楼下，一扇缺了部分玻璃的窗透进光。窗帘飘过那块空处。碎裂声并不是你想出来的。你碰到窗下的墙，湿气让灰泥很凉。袖子突然被锋利的东西勾住，你缩回手，看见一小片三角玻璃嵌在布里。先把它留在那里，等看清该往哪边拔。
          <br><br>
          厨房里没有人。来客的座位已经收好，你的盘子却仍在桌上，食物还推在中央。你无法确定，这是第二天早晨，还是几个晚上被挤进了同一个早晨。水槽旁有只杯子，你翻过来检查。里面是干净的，把手却湿着。
          <br><br>
          你往杯里灌水，因为端水是你知道怎样做的事。父母要是病了，可能会需要。要是生气，送水也许能让第一句话容易出口。明明用了冷水，杯子却烫着手指。你放下，等一等，再拿起。这次凉了。你分不出哪一次感觉出了错，只在下面垫一块布。不是因为一定会洒，而是让发抖的手指有地方藏。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-upstairs'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The water rises towards the rim at every step. You walk slower, watching the cup so closely that you reach your parents' door without looking at the passage. The door is already open. Inside, the furniture has been torn apart, except for the bed. You stop with one foot raised. The water spills over your fingers before you remember to put the foot down.
          <br><br>
          Something breathes beneath the bed. The sound catches, then begins again with a wet pull. You say that you have brought water. You do not say who you brought it for. You want the person who answers to decide that. A hand comes out far enough to touch the edge of a fallen sheet. The fingers are long and the nails shine silver. You have seen the hand before. That is worse than not recognising it.
          <br><br>
          A pale face shows for a moment beneath the frame. It is thinner than it should be. The eyes do not look towards the cup. They look at the fingers holding it. You step back, and the hand moves with you, dragging cloth until a torn corner catches on a nail. You cannot see whether there is another body behind the first. You cannot make yourself bend low enough to find out.
          <br><br>
          You hear yourself asking, "Who did this?" The hand stops. You wait for your parent to say your name, to tell you that you should not be here. A noise comes from beneath the bed, low and wet. <span class='red'>The fingers are still reaching. The empty space beside them is not a safe place to kneel.</span> You set the cup outside their reach and move backwards, keeping your face towards the bed until the doorframe is between you and it.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          每上一级台阶，水就朝杯沿涌。你越走越慢，只顾看杯子，还没看过道，就已经到了父母门前。门开着。屋里的家具都被撕碎了，只有床完好。你抬着一只脚停住，直到水淌过手指，才记起应该把脚落下。
          <br><br>
          床下有东西在呼吸。声音卡住，接着湿湿地抽了一口气。你说，送水来了，却没有说是给谁的。你希望让回答的人来决定。一只手伸出来，碰到垂落的床单。手指很长，指甲泛着银光。你见过这只手。认得它，比不认得它更糟。
          <br><br>
          床架下短暂露出一张苍白的脸，瘦得不对。眼睛不看杯子，只看端杯子的手指。你后退，那只手也跟着动，拖起一角布，直到破角挂在指甲上。你看不见后面还有没有另一具身体，也无法让自己弯得够低去确认。
          <br><br>
          你听见自己问：“是谁干的？”手停住了。你等父母喊你的名字，说你不该待在这里。床下却只发出低低的、湿乎乎的声音。<span class='red'>手指仍在够过来，它们旁边的空处不适合跪下。</span>你把杯子放在够不到的位置，慢慢倒退，始终面对着床，直到门框隔在你与它之间。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-linen'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          In the cupboard, towels have fallen into a pile. You pull one out and find a thin line of glass resting in its fold. It looks like water until you tilt it. You put the towel down again. There are so many ways to cut yourself in a room that used to contain nothing sharper than the edge of a drawer.
          <br><br>
          Your hand has begun to sting. A small scrape runs beside the thumb. You look back towards your parents' door, then at the cloth beneath the cup. Perhaps the cup had a chipped edge. Perhaps you caught it on the window downstairs. You pour a little water over the scrape and bite your lip while it stings.
          <br><br>
          You find a folded strip without glass in it. The smell is the same as the linen used on your bed. For a moment you are in an ordinary morning, complaining because a shirt has been laid out that you do not like. Your parent is telling you to put it on anyway. You want that argument back so badly that you begin to answer it. The cupboard does not answer. A piece of torn wood settles somewhere nearby.
          <br><br>
          You can wrap the hand and carry things more safely, or leave the sharp pieces alone until you have room to see. There is no adult watching how you do it. Yesterday you would have been pleased by that. Today you wish someone would tell you that you are making a fuss over a little scratch. You pull the cupboard door mostly shut and leave a finger's width of light. Then you widen the gap again. You do not want anything else in the house learning how easily you make a room dark.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          柜子里，毛巾掉成了一堆。你抽出一条，发现折缝里躺着细细一线玻璃。原先像水，歪一下才看清。你又把毛巾放下。以前，这间屋里最锋利的不过是抽屉边，现在却有太多能割伤自己的东西。
          <br><br>
          你的手开始刺痛，拇指旁有一道小擦伤。你先看父母的门，再看杯子下面的布。也许杯沿有缺口，也许是楼下窗边刮到的。你倒出一点水冲伤口，疼得咬住了嘴唇。
          <br><br>
          你找到了没有玻璃的叠布。味道与床上的被单一样。一瞬间，又回到普通早晨，你抱怨不喜欢摆出来的衣服，父母叫你照穿。你太想要那场争吵回来，已经开始回答。柜子没有回应，附近某处，一块撕碎的木头落稳了。
          <br><br>
          可以先包住手，更安全地搬东西。也可以暂时不碰锋利碎片，等有地方看清再处理。没有大人盯着你怎么做。昨天还会为此高兴，今天却希望有人说，你为小擦伤大惊小怪。你把柜门推得只剩一指宽的光，随后又推开一些。不希望这房子里别的东西也学会，你把一个房间弄暗有多容易。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-copy'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-wrapped')>>You wrapped your hand before carrying anything else. The cloth is still there, and it makes the pencil awkward to hold.<<else>>In your room, you put a sheet on the desk.<</if>> You are going to write down what the adults said. <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('draw')>>You have drawn this house before, on paper with room for every window. These lines will not hold a window.<</if>> Not a letter to anybody. Just something that will stay the same when you look away. You have paper, a pencil, and room for several lines. That ought to be enough for the little amount you really heard.
          <br><br>
          The first line is easy. We agreed. You remember the pause afterwards more clearly than the voice. You put a space beneath it. Before anyone changes their mind comes next, though you cannot swear it came next in the room. Not in front of. Your name. You keep the pieces apart. When you look up from the desk, the spaces seem too large. The words are trying to become one sentence even without your help.
          <br><br>
          A faint sound comes through the wall. For a moment it supplies exactly what you need. Not in front of the child because the child will tell. You put the pencil down. You did not hear because. You did not hear tell. You heard something beneath the bed and have been giving it words ever since. The page has no way of telling you which words came from a mouth and which came from you.
          <br><br>
          You want to leave the uncertain parts empty. Empty paper looks stupid, like work you have not finished. You imagine an adult taking it, tapping the spaces, telling you to make an effort. You can nearly hear that voice now, though you do not know whose it is. <span class='teal'>Keeping several possibilities apart takes enough doubt. If you cannot, the dream may fill a gap before you notice.</span> You pick up the pencil again. The point is blunter than when you put it down. There is a mark beneath your hand that you do not remember making.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-wrapped')>>搬别的东西以前，你先包住了手。布还在，握笔变得别扭。<<else>>回到房间，你把纸铺在桌上，<</if>>准备记下大人说过的话。<<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('draw')>>你以前画过这所房子，纸上有位置放下每一扇窗。这几行字放不下一扇。<</if>>不是写信给谁，只想留下一个在移开目光后不会改变的东西。你有纸，有铅笔，也有足够写几行的位置。应该能放下真正听见的那一点。
          <br><br>
          第一行很容易。说好了的。声音记不清，后面的停顿却记得。你留一段空白。趁还没人改主意，放在下一行，却不能保证屋里也是这个顺序。别当着。你的名字。你尽量把碎片分开。抬头再看，空隙却显得太大。那些词想变成一句话，甚至不需要你帮忙。
          <br><br>
          墙里传来轻响，一瞬间刚好补出需要的部分。别当着孩子，因为孩子会说出去。你放下笔。你没有听见因为，也没听见说出去。你只听见床下的东西，却从那以后一直替它添词。纸没办法告诉你，哪些话来自一张嘴，哪些来自自己。
          <br><br>
          你想把不确定的地方空着。空白看起来却很蠢，像没做完的作业。你想象大人拿起纸，敲敲空处，叫你用点心。几乎听见声音，又不知道是谁。<span class='teal'>需要保留足够的疑心，才能不把几种可能混在一起。做不到时，梦可能赶在你发现前填满空缺。</span>你再次拿笔，笔尖比放下时钝。手下有一道不记得写过的痕迹。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-gap'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You leave a space where because wanted to go. You read the broken sentence aloud, then stop. It sounds wrong. You draw a short line on either side of the gap so that you will know you left it deliberately. The marks look like a little doorway.
          <br><br>
          From the hall, something scrapes against the wall. You keep your pencil still until it stops. The gap is still there when you look back. For once, a thing has remained what you decided it was. You put a finger over it. The paper is cooler in the empty place than beneath the writing, although that cannot be true. You lift the finger and check the desk underneath. There is no hole.
          <br><br>
          You can remember the expression on your parent's face when the conversation stopped. That is worth writing too. You try, and discover you do not have a word for the expression. Angry would be easier. Frightened would be easier. It was the face of somebody hearing a sound they had expected, at a time they had not expected it. You do not know whether that memory belongs to the landing or the supper table.
          <br><br>
          You write both places on separate lines. Your hand aches from holding the pencil too tightly. The hand under the bed was reaching for you. You can write that. You cannot write what the voice meant. You fold the edge of the page, then unfold it before the crease crosses the gap. When you find somebody to tell, you will show them the paper.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你把因为想挤进去的地方空着。你试着把断开的句子念出来，又停住，听起来不对。你在空处两边各画一道短线，好提醒自己，这是故意留下的。两道线像一扇小门。
          <br><br>
          过道里有东西刮着墙。你握笔不动，等声音停下，再看纸。空缺还在。终于有一件事，保持着你决定的样子。你用手指盖住，发现空处比字下面凉，明明不该如此。抬手检查桌子，没有洞。
          <br><br>
          你想得起谈话停止时父母的表情，觉得也值得记。试着写，才发现没有能用的词。生气容易，害怕也容易。那是一个人听见自己早就料到、却没想到会在这时候响起的声音时，会有的脸。你分不清这段记忆来自楼梯口，还是晚饭桌。
          <br><br>
          你把两个地方分成两行写下。握笔太紧，手在痛。床下的手确实在够你，这一句写得出。可那个声音是什么意思，你写不出。你折起纸边，折痕快要穿过空缺时又展开。等找到能说的人，就把纸拿给对方看。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-complete'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The sentence is finished. You have written the words that were missing and drawn a line beneath them. For a moment you feel much better. There is a reason. A reason can be remembered. You read the line twice, finding it easier the second time, and only then notice that you cannot hear the voice that gave you its middle.
          <br><br>
          You remember your pencil moving. You remember deciding where to put because. You do not remember anybody saying what came after it. The relief begins to turn into something hot in your face. You have done the thing you were angry at the adults for doing: answered a question different from the one you actually heard.
          <br><br>
          You put a line through the middle words. The graphite smears beneath your little finger. The sentence remains readable under the line, which makes you want to cross it out harder. You nearly tear the paper before stopping. Something in the passage sounds like a pleased breath. You lift your head. It could be air through the damaged window. It could be somebody waiting to see whether you will throw the page away.
          <br><br>
          You keep the paper. When you try to rub out the wrong words, the page begins to tear, so you leave them crossed out. You draw a lighter line beneath the words you did hear. <span class='teal'>The completed sentence was not something you witnessed. Crossing it out gives you another way to continue.</span> You turn the page over. The pencil has left a dark patch on the back. You cover it with your thumb before it can look any more like a person bending over a bed.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          句子已经写完。你补出缺少的词，还在下面划了线。一瞬间好受得多。有了理由，而理由可以记住。你读两遍，第二遍更顺，随后才发现，中间那一段根本想不起是谁的声音。
          <br><br>
          你记得笔在移动，记得决定把因为放在哪里，却不记得有谁说过它后面的内容。安心变成脸上一点热。你做了刚才责怪大人的那件事，回答的不是实际听见的问题。
          <br><br>
          你划掉中间的词，石墨在小指下晕开。线下面仍读得出，让你想更用力地划。差点弄破纸，才停住。过道像传来满意的呼气。你抬头，那可能只是坏窗吹进的风，也可能有人在等，看你会不会把纸扔掉。
          <br><br>
          你留着纸。想擦掉错的字时，纸快要破了，只好留着那些划线。你在真正听见的词下面，再划一条轻些的线。<span class='teal'>补全的句子不是亲眼见证的事。把它划掉，仍可以换一种方式往下走。</span>你翻过纸，笔压在背面留下黑影。它越来越像一个弯在床边的人，你用拇指把那块盖住。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-script'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The back of the page contains a shopping list. You did not write it. The handwriting looks like your parent's, until you put the front beside a note left on the desk. The same letters lean in opposite directions. Bread, soap, something you cannot read. The unreadable item is underlined so often that the paper is worn thin.
          <br><br>
          You try to remember whether the sheet was used before you took it. You have a picture of blank paper. You also have a picture of this exact list beside the fruit bowl. Neither picture agrees to go away. You rub the worn place with your thumb. A tiny hole opens, and light from the window shines through. At least the hole is a thing you can check from both sides.
          <br><br>
          Beyond your door, a parent calls you downstairs. The voice is normal. You almost run to it. Then you hear the same call again, with the same breath before your name. Not another call made by a person getting impatient. The same call, copied. You wait for it a third time. It does not come. You cannot decide whether waiting made it stop or whether the silence is waiting for you instead.
          <br><br>
          You open the window a little. Cold air moves the page, and the list turns towards the glass. Your unfinished account faces you. You check the shopping list again. The last word is still unreadable. You pin a corner beneath the book, leaving the little hole uncovered. Outside, a neighbour pushes a bin back through a gate. You listen to the wheels rattle until the gate shuts.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          纸背面有张购物单，不是你写的。笔迹像父母，直到你拿来与桌上的字条对比。同样的字母，倾斜方向却相反。面包，肥皂，还有读不出的东西。最后一项划了太多次线，纸已经磨薄。
          <br><br>
          你努力想，取来以前是不是用过这张纸。脑子里有一张白纸，也有这份购物单放在水果碗旁的画面。两幅都不肯消失。你用拇指揉磨薄的位置，开出一个小洞，窗光透进来。至少，洞是可以从两面检查的东西。
          <br><br>
          门外，父母喊你下楼。声音正常，你差点跑过去。随后又听见一次，名字前的换气都相同。不是一个人不耐烦才又喊，更像同一声被复制。你等第三次，却没有了。你分不清是等候让它停下，还是安静也在等你。
          <br><br>
          你把窗推开一点，冷风掀动纸，购物单翻向玻璃，自己的未完记录对着你。你再看一遍购物单，最后那个词仍读不出。你把一角压在书下，不盖住小洞。外面，邻居把垃圾桶推回院门。你一直听着轮子咔啦响，直到门关上。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-morning'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You wake on the chair with the book against your cheek. The paper is still beneath it. There is a line on your face where the cover pressed. You touch it and are glad to feel something whose cause you know. The light outside is brighter. The house has not repaired itself while you slept.
          <br><br>
          You have to eat. The thought arrives with an ache, not a question. Downstairs, you find food that needs no knife and sit with your back towards the wall. The empty visitor's chair is in the wrong place. You move it aside with your foot. It is heavy, exactly as heavy as a chair should be. You do not try to remember who last sat in it until after you have swallowed.
          <br><br>
          From upstairs comes a short dragging sound. You know which direction it comes from. That is enough knowledge for now. You put the rest of your breakfast out of reach of the open window and decide to go out while the street is busy. You do not have a brave speech prepared. There are ordinary things the kitchen needs. You can buy them, or ask about them, without first explaining every broken part of the house.
          <br><br>
          The key on its nail has not changed. It fits the lock. You test the door twice before stepping through it. The second test makes you feel foolish, then safer, then foolish again. A passer-by glances at you and keeps walking. They have seen a child at a gate, not a person with a terrible story. For a moment you are grateful. Then you want them to look back. The shopping list is folded in your pocket with your account on the other side. Outside this house, you might be able to tell which side of the paper belongs to which morning.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你在椅子上醒来，书抵着脸，纸还在下面。书皮压出一道印。摸到那道印，反而有点高兴，这是知道原因的东西。外面比先前更亮，房子没有趁你睡着时修复自己。
          <br><br>
          你得吃东西。念头伴着肚子痛到来，不是问题。楼下有不用刀的食物，你背靠墙坐下。来客的空椅子位置不对，于是用脚挪开。很沉，恰好是椅子该有的重量。直到咽下第一口，才去想上一次谁坐过。
          <br><br>
          楼上传来短短的拖动声。你知道哪个方向，暂时知道这些就够。你把剩下的早餐挪到开窗够不到的位置，决定趁街上人多出门。不必先准备勇敢的话。厨房缺普通东西，可以买，也可以问，不用先解释屋里每一处坏掉的地方。
          <br><br>
          钉子上的钥匙没变，能开锁。跨出去前，你试门两次。第二次让你先觉得蠢，再觉得安全，然后又蠢。路人看一眼便继续走，看到的是门口的孩子，不是一个装着可怕故事的人。一瞬间你感谢对方，接着又想让对方回头。购物单折在口袋，另一面是自己的记录。到了房子外，也许能分清哪一面属于哪一个早晨。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-ticket'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          In the street, you find a little paper receipt beside your shoe. It is wet at one end. You almost pick it up, then keep your hands in your pockets and step over it. The next house has a clean doorstep and somebody laughing inside. That sound makes you angry before you know why.
          <br><br>
          You count houses to keep track of where you are. The numbers on the gates seem to change when you look between them. One could be a three or an eight. Another has a screw where the middle should be. Counting the actual houses is easier. You can see their walls. You cannot make an extra wall appear merely because you would like the count to end sooner.
          <br><br>
          At the corner, a shopkeeper is putting out a board. You wait until it is safely on the pavement before passing. The person thanks you without looking closely, and you nearly begin telling them about the hand under the bed. The words are ready. Then you picture the conversation becoming a different one: whose house, which parents, have you been watching frightening things, go home and ask an adult. You walk on with the words still in your mouth.
          <br><br>
          Across the road, you see someone who might be Sydney. You recognise the way the person carries a bag before you can see the face. You start forward, then stop at the kerb. You have run towards people before and been wrong. Traffic passes between you. When it has gone, the person is still there, adjusting the strap. You walk along the pavement for a better look.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          街上，鞋旁有张小收据，一头湿了。你差点伸手，又把手留在口袋里，从上面跨过去。下一所房子的门阶很干净，里面有人笑。你还不知道为什么，就先感到生气。
          <br><br>
          你数房子，好知道走到了哪里。来回看时，大门号码像会变。有一个可能是三，也可能是八，另一个中间装着螺丝。直接数房子更容易，你看得见它们的墙。不能只是希望早点数完，就让另一堵墙出现。
          <br><br>
          转角，店主正在搬告示板。你等板子在地上放稳才经过，对方没仔细看便道谢。你差点开始说床下那只手，话已经准备好。接着又想象谈话变成另一些问题。哪所房子，谁的父母，是不是看了吓人的东西，回家问大人。你继续走，话仍留在嘴里。
          <br><br>
          马路对面，有人可能是悉尼。还没看清脸，你就认出拿包的方式。你急着向前，却在路沿停住。以前跑向人，也认错过。车从中间经过，驶走后，那人仍在调整背带。你沿人行道走，想再看清一点。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-outside'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('garden') or $LostLamb.marks.includes('flower')>>You know this street from the other end, where it turns towards the garden and the narrow path. Sydney is standing on the part you never had to walk alone.<</if>>
          It is Sydney. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> notices you before you reach the same side of the street. There is a little lift of the hand, then the hand goes back to the bag. You remember <<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>> waving through the window. This is not that afternoon. You tell yourself not to make it into that afternoon just because you would like to.
          <br><br>
          Sydney asks whether you are shopping. You show the folded paper without opening it. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> says something about being sent out too. You can talk about bread for several moments without anybody telling you to go upstairs. Ordinary conversation makes your shoulders loosen. You had not noticed how high you were holding them.
          <br><br>
          You want to ask whether Sydney has been told about your parents. Before you can, <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> looks towards someone farther along the pavement. You cannot see who is there because a parked vehicle blocks the view. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> looks back at you and says your name very quietly. The quietness puts you beside the connecting door again. You step away from the kerb, as though distance from the traffic might also put distance between the two scenes.
          <br><br>
          A person is carrying a paper bag past you. Its bottom has begun to tear. You notice the tear and want to warn them, but Sydney is speaking now and you do not want to miss another sentence. An apple falls. The person catches the bag before the rest follow. Nobody is hurt. You watch the apple roll to a gutter and wonder how many little things you have failed to notice while trying to hear something larger. Sydney asks whether you are all right. You say you wanted to ask a question. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> says, "I know. You always do." Then <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> looks away, as if that was more than <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> meant to say.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('garden') or $LostLamb.marks.includes('flower')>>这条街你从另一头认得，那里拐向花园和那条窄路。悉尼站的这一段，你以前从不必一个人走。<</if>>
          是悉尼。还没走到街道同一侧，<<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>>就看见你。手稍稍抬起，又放回包上。你记得窗外挥手的样子，可这不是那个下午。你告诉自己，不能因为想要，就把它改成那个下午。
          <br><br>
          悉尼问你是不是买东西。你给<<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>>看折起来的纸，没有打开。<<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>>说自己也被派出来。你们能谈几句面包，没有人叫你上楼。普通的交谈让肩膀松了。先前都没发现，自己一直把肩耸得那么高。
          <br><br>
          你想问，有没有人告诉悉尼关于父母的事。还没来得及，<<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>>就看向更远的一个人。停着的车挡住了视线，你看不见是谁。再看回你时，<<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>>很轻地叫名字。那份轻又把你放回连通门边。你退离路沿，像离车远些，也能让两个场景分开。
          <br><br>
          有人提着纸袋经过，底部开始裂开。你想提醒，悉尼却正在说话，不愿再漏一句。一个苹果掉出来，对方及时托住袋底，其余没掉，没人受伤。你看着苹果滚到沟边，想着自己试图听清一件大事时，漏掉了多少小事。悉尼问你还好吗。你说想问个问题。<<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>>说：“我知道。你每次都这样。”随后别开脸，像说多了。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-sydney'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          Sydney says <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> is not allowed to come to your house now. You have heard that in several different ways already. Hearing it from <<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>> does not make it softer. You ask what has changed. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> presses the bag against <<nnpc_his 'Sydney'>> leg and says <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> has been told not to. It is an answer, but it is not the answer you wanted.
          <br><br>
          You tell <<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>> that the adults are keeping something from you. Sydney looks down the pavement again. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> knows that adults keep things from children. That is not the same as knowing what this particular thing is. You want <<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>> to be angry with you, on your side, at the right people. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> is worried instead. The worry might be about you. It might be about getting back late. You cannot make it choose.
          <br><br>
          Somebody calls Sydney. The name travels round the parked vehicle. You still cannot see the person who called. Sydney makes a small apologetic face. You recognise that expression well enough to dislike seeing it now. You could let <<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>> leave, which would mean accepting that the conversation has not solved anything. You could demand one clear reason. You know how easy it is for a demand to sound like a promise that someone will finally give you what you want.
          <br><br>
          You almost say that something happened upstairs. The words would change this conversation, and you cannot be certain what they would change it into. You keep them behind your teeth. Sydney says, "I didn't decide it." You believe that and are still hurt. Both things can happen at once. The person out of sight calls again, less patiently. The ordinary noise of a street continues around you, without slowing down to let either of you find a better ending to the conversation.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          悉尼说，现在不许<<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>>去你家。你已经用不同方式听过几次，从<<nnpc_his 'Sydney'>>嘴里听见，也没变轻。你问变了什么。<<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>>把包按在腿边，说有人交代不能去。这是回答，却不是你想要的回答。
          <br><br>
          你说，大人在瞒一件事。悉尼又看人行道深处。<<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>>知道大人会瞒孩子，不等于知道这次究竟瞒什么。你希望<<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>>和你一起生气，站在你这边，针对该针对的人。<<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>>却在担心，可能担心你，也可能担心回去晚。你无法逼它只选一种。
          <br><br>
          有人喊悉尼，名字绕过停着的车，你仍看不见喊的人。悉尼露出一点歉意。这个熟悉的表情，现在反而不想看。你可以让<<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>>走，意味着谈话什么也没解决。也可以坚持要一个明确理由。你知道，要求很容易听起来像终于能得到想要东西的保证。
          <br><br>
          你差点说，楼上发生了事。那会改变谈话，却不能保证变成什么。你把话留在牙后。悉尼说：“不是我决定的。”你相信，仍然难过，两件事可以同时发生。看不见的人又喊了一次，更不耐烦。街道的普通声音围着你们继续，没有放慢，好让谁替谈话找到更好的结尾。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-return'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-demanded-sydney')>>You ask for a reason before Sydney leaves. <<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>> says <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> has already told you what <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> was told. The second answer is shorter, and you know your demand has made it shorter. You want to take the demand back without taking the question back. There is no way to do both before the voice calls again.<<else>>You let Sydney go. It is not forgiveness. You have not decided there is anything to forgive. You step aside so <<nnpc_he 'Sydney'>> can pass, and find that a little ordinary movement can hurt without anybody doing it cruelly.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          At the shop, you buy what you can understand on the list. There is a mistake with the change, or you think there is. The shopkeeper counts the coins in your palm and shows you each one. This answer is wonderfully small. You can see where it begins and ends. You nearly ask them to count the other things troubling you in the same way.
          <br><br>
          You carry bread and soap back. The paper bag is stronger than the one you watched tear, but you hold a hand beneath it anyway. At your gate, the key sticks until you stop pushing against the door. Once you ease the pressure, it turns. You wish somebody would tell you which way to ease the pressure inside the house.
          <br><br>
          The kitchen is lit when you enter. There are voices beyond it. One of them belongs to your parent. You stop with the shopping against your chest. Upstairs, behind the door you left mostly open, something drags over the floor. The two sounds should not belong to the same person at the same time. You could decide that one is a memory. You could decide that one is a lie. You stand still instead, until somebody in the kitchen asks what you have bought. Then you lift the bag like a person who has come home from shopping and has nothing stranger to explain.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-demanded-sydney')>>你要悉尼走前给一个理由。<<nnpc_He 'Sydney'>>说，已经把被告知的话都告诉你了。第二次回答更短，你知道是自己的要求让它变短。你想收回要求，却不收回问题。声音再次催促以前，没办法两样都做到。<<else>>你让悉尼走。这不是原谅，你还没决定有什么需要原谅。只是侧身让<<nnpc_him 'Sydney'>>经过，却发现一个小小的普通动作也能让人痛，不必是谁故意残酷。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          在商店里，你买下购物单上能看懂的东西。找零出了错，也可能只是你以为。店主在掌心数硬币，一枚枚指出来。这个答案小得太好，你能看见开头与结束。你差点想请对方用同样的方法数一数，别的那些困扰。
          <br><br>
          你提着面包和肥皂回来。纸袋比刚才那只结实，你仍把手垫在底下。到了自家大门，钥匙卡住，直到你不再用力推门。压力松开，就能转动。你希望有人说清，屋里的压力又该朝哪边松。
          <br><br>
          进门时厨房亮着，后面有说话声，其中一个属于父母。你抱着购物袋停下。楼上，那道故意没关严的门后，有东西拖过地面。这两种声音不该在同一刻属于同一个人。你可以认定一个是记忆，也可以认定一个在骗你。你却先站着，直到厨房有人问买了什么。才举起袋子，像只是买东西回家，没有更奇怪的事需要解释。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-kitchen-entry'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('kitchen') or $LostLamb.marks.includes('helped')>>You know this kitchen. You were shown where the cups go, and you put the shopping down in the right place without being told.<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('tasted')>>You have taken food from a counter before, when nobody was looking. Tonight you wait to be told.<<else>>You set the shopping on the table.<</if>> The soap has made the bread smell faintly of something you would wash from your hands. You separate them. Your parent says you should have done that at the shop. The complaint is so familiar that you almost laugh. Instead you move the soap to the sink, very carefully, as though the familiar voice might disappear if you make the wrong noise.
          <br><br>
          There is company in the next room. You can hear the quieter voice from the landing again. The kitchen door has been wedged open, but the connecting door farther along is closed. The sound seems to come through the closed one. You do not point this out. You are told there is food to warm and cups to wash. There will be time to talk after supper.
          <br><br>
          After supper sounds like a place rather than a time. A place you have been trying to reach since Sydney stopped coming. You look at the clock, which is working. You watch the hand make one small movement to be sure. If you do the tasks quietly, perhaps the conversation will happen without the adults deciding to stop it. If you listen closely, you might learn enough not to need their permission.
          <br><br>
          The stove is not ready. You can work slowly there and let your breathing settle. Cups have been left beside the sink, near enough to the passage that water would not quite cover the voices. A bowl is on the top pantry shelf, and the stool needs moving to reach it. None of these is a secret object. They are things a child has been told to help with. <span class='blue'>Preparing one part of supper is enough to carry it through. Extra tasks can change your fear and the attention on you, but the evening keeps moving.</span> You push your sleeves back and choose where to begin.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('kitchen') or $LostLamb.marks.includes('helped')>>这间厨房你熟。有人教过你杯子该放哪里，你把买来的东西也放在对的位置，不必人提醒。<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('tasted')>>你曾在没人看的时候从柜台上拿过吃的。今晚你等着被交代。<<else>>你把购物放上桌。<</if>>肥皂让面包带着一点本该从手上洗掉的味道，于是把它们分开。父母说，商店里就该这样做。抱怨熟悉得让你差点笑出来，最后却很小心地把肥皂挪到水槽，仿佛声音弄错，熟悉的嗓音就会消失。
          <br><br>
          隔壁有客人，你又听见楼梯口那个轻声的来客。厨房门撑开，更深处的连通门却关着，声音像从关着的门来。你没有指出这一点。有人交代，食物要热，杯子要洗，晚饭后有时间谈。
          <br><br>
          晚饭后更像地方，不像时间。悉尼不再来以后，你就一直在试着抵达。你看钟，它还在走，盯着指针挪一小下，好确认。要是把活做得安静，谈话也许能发生，不被大人决定停止。要是听得够仔细，也许就能知道得足够多，不再需要允许。
          <br><br>
          炉子还没准备好。你可以在那里慢慢做，让呼吸平稳。水槽边有杯子，离过道够近，流水不至于盖住全部声音。储藏架最高处有碗，得挪凳子才够得到。都不是秘密道具，只是孩子被交代帮忙的事。<span class='blue'>准备好晚饭的一部分，就可以端过去。额外的活会改变不安与受到的注意，但晚上一直在往前走。</span>你推起袖子，选择从哪里开始。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-kitchen'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-kitchen-return')>>You come back to the middle of the kitchen. The shopping has been put away. <<if Time.hour lt 17>>The voices are still close enough to hear by the sink, but they will move before five.<<else>>The adults have moved away from the sink side of the room. The cups no longer offer a place to listen.<</if>> <span class='blue'>You can take supper through now. Another task is optional, and each can be done only once.</span> The stove offers something slow enough to finish without being watched. The pantry gives you room to turn your back for a moment.<<else>>The floor is clear between the stove and the table. You check it before moving, though there was nothing there a moment ago. A little flour has spilled at the edge of the cupboard. It shows one narrow track, as though somebody pulled a fingertip through it. You follow the track to a knot in the wood and stop. Ordinary flour does not need to become a message.
          <br><br>
          Your parent tells you to mind the heat. You say you will. The warning leaves you with something you can do correctly. You remember standing here on a different evening while someone corrected how you held a spoon. That memory contains no visitor. You try to keep it nearby while taking the spoon from the drawer.
          <br><br>
          From the next room comes the word willing. It is followed by a pause that makes you stop moving. Willing to what? Willing when? You know adults can ask questions in a way that makes yes the only answer they recognise. You have said yes to things because you wanted the asking to end. It is not a thought a child should have. You do not know where it came from.
          <br><br>
          You put the spoon down before the heat reaches your fingers. There are tasks, and there is the desire to listen. You can make one serve the other, but doing so might make somebody look up. <span class='blue'>The sink is a listening place only before five. Choose the cups first to use it. Slow work by the stove can settle you. The pantry gives a different view.</span><</if>>
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-kitchen-return')>>你回到厨房中间，买来的东西已经收好。<<if Time.hour lt 17>>声音仍离水槽很近，五点以后，大人就会挪到别处。<<else>>大人已经离开水槽附近。杯子不再给你一个能偷听的位置。<</if>><span class='blue'>现在就能把晚饭端过去。再做一件活是可选的，每件只能做一次。</span>炉边的事可以慢慢完成，不必一直被盯着。储藏架让你有机会短暂转过身。<<else>>炉子与桌子之间，地板仍然空着。你先检查才走，尽管刚才那里什么也没有。柜边洒着一点面粉，只有一条窄痕，像被指尖拖过。你顺着看，到了木头节疤就停下。普通的面粉不需要变成一则消息。
          <br><br>
          父母叫你当心热，你说会的。这个提醒给了一件能够正确做到的事。你记起另一个晚上，有人在这里纠正拿勺子的姿势，记忆里没有来客。取出勺子时，你试着把这份记忆留得近一些。
          <br><br>
          隔壁说出自愿。后面的停顿让你停止动作。自愿做什么，什么时候自愿。你知道，大人有一种问法，只认“是”这一个答案。你以前为了让提问结束，也答过是。这不是孩子会有的念头。你不知道它从哪来。
          <br><br>
          热还没传到手指，你先放下勺子。一边是要做的活，一边是想听。可以让前者帮助后者，却也可能引得谁抬头。<span class='blue'>只有五点前能在水槽边听见谈话。想利用它，得先选洗杯子。炉边慢慢做能平稳不安，储藏架则给了另一个角度。</span><</if>>
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-stove'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You set the pan where you have been shown to set it and turn the heat low. The handle stays turned away from the edge. These are rules with reasons attached. A hot handle can burn you. A pan near the edge can fall. Remembering them is a relief after listening to rules whose reasons seem to move around the house.
          <br><br>
          You stir slowly. The food resists the spoon, loosens, then resists again. You follow the changes rather than the voices. There are little steam marks on the tiles. A scratch beside the stove catches the light. You remember making it with something you were not supposed to carry through the kitchen. Nobody remembers the argument anymore, or nobody mentions it. Not every thing that happened has been hidden deliberately.
          <br><br>
          The quieter voice says something in the next room. You do not turn. Your hands are occupied, and the heat gives you a reason to pay attention to them. For several breaths you manage not to put the missing words together. The fear does not disappear. It becomes a thing happening beside you instead of something taking up the whole kitchen.
          <br><br>
          Your parent comes near enough to check the pan. You make room without leaving the stove. A hand reaches past your shoulder. For a moment you expect silver nails. The nails are ordinary, cut short, with a little dirt beside one. You stare at them too long. Your parent asks whether you have burnt yourself. You shake your head. The hand is withdrawn before you can decide what its ordinariness proves. You have seen another hand under the bed. Both pictures remain. The food is ready now. You turn the heat down and feel the spoon settle against the side of the pan. You can carry this part of supper without first solving the rest of the house.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你按教过的位置放锅，调低火，把柄转得远离边沿。这些规则带着理由。热柄会烫，靠边的锅会掉。听了许多理由在屋里移来移去的规则以后，能记起这些，让人松口气。
          <br><br>
          你慢慢搅动。食物抵住勺子，松开，又抵住。你留心这些变化，不留心声音。瓷砖上有细细的蒸汽痕，炉旁一道划伤映着光。你记得，是自己带着不该穿过厨房的东西划的。没人再记那场争吵，或只是没人提。并不是每一件发生过的事都故意被藏起来。
          <br><br>
          隔壁轻声说了什么，你没有转头。手上在忙，热也给了留心双手的理由。有几次呼吸，你不再拼那些没听见的词。害怕没有消失，却成了身旁发生的东西，不再挤满整间厨房。
          <br><br>
          父母靠近检查锅。你腾出位置，没有离开炉边。一只手越过肩膀，一瞬间，你以为会有银指甲。指甲却普通，剪得短，旁边有一点污。你看得太久，被问是不是烫伤，只摇头。还没决定普通的手能证明什么，手就收回了。床下那只手也见过，两幅画面仍然留着。食物热好了。你调小火，感到勺子贴稳锅边。这份晚饭，可以不用先解开房子的其余部分就端走。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-cups'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You wash one cup, then another. The water makes enough noise to cover small movements, not enough to cover the adults. You angle a cup beneath the tap so that it fills slowly. While it fills, you can stand still with a reason. The trick is so simple that you wonder whether they know it already.
          <br><br>
          The visitor says, "They knew what they were asking." Your parent answers something you cannot hear. You turn the tap lower. A floorboard sounds behind you. You straighten at once and tip the water away. Your parent asks why you are wasting it. You cannot answer that you were saving the quiet parts between the splashes.
          <br><br>
          You are told to finish. The cups are clean, but you wash the same one again while deciding whether to ask who they mean. Asking would expose what you heard. Not asking leaves the pronoun ready to fit anybody. It could mean your parents. It could mean people whose faces you have never seen. You try fitting it to the visitor, and the clean cuff appears in the water beneath your hands.
          <br><br>
          You look round. Nobody is close enough for their cuff to be reflected in the sink. The dark shape dissolves when you move the cup, becoming your own sleeve in the wrong place. You push both sleeves higher. A little mark beside your thumb catches the soap. It stings and makes the sink ordinary again for a moment. You rinse, set the last cup upside down, and leave the tap off. You have heard another phrase, <span class='red'>but listening here has drawn attention to you.</span> Your parent tells the visitor you are old enough to help. The praise feels like another instruction to remain where they can see your hands.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你洗一只杯子，再洗一只。水声够遮住小动作，不够遮住大人的声音。你把杯子倾在龙头下，让它慢慢灌满。灌着的时候，就有理由站着不动。办法简单得让你怀疑，他们是不是早就知道。
          <br><br>
          来客说：“他们知道自己在要求什么。”父母答了句什么，你听不见。你把水调小。背后有木板响。你立刻直起身，把水倒掉，被问为什么浪费。你不能回答，自己在收集水花之间安静的部分。
          <br><br>
          他们叫你快做完。杯子已经干净，你却又洗同一只，犹豫要不要问他们是谁。问会暴露刚听见的话，不问，代词就能套进任何人。可能是父母，可能是从没见过脸的人。你试着套给来客，干净的袖口就在手下的水里浮出。
          <br><br>
          你回头，没有人近得能把袖口映进水槽。挪动杯子，黑影散了，变成一只位置不对的自己的袖子。你把两只袖子都推高。拇指边的小伤碰到肥皂，刺痛让水槽暂时又普通。你冲净杯子，把最后一只倒扣，关好水。你又听见了一截话，但<span class='red'>在这里偷听，已经引起注意。</span>父母告诉来客，你大得能帮忙。夸奖更像指令，要留在他们看得见双手的位置。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-pantry'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You move the stool a little at a time so its legs do not scrape. It is heavier than you expected. You would like somebody to help with it, but help would put another person beside you. You are trying to have a moment in which nobody is deciding where you should stand.
          <br><br>
          From the stool you can see over the counter. In the next room, part of the visitor's chair appears beyond the doorframe. A hand rests on the arm. There is a cup on the table beside it. You look between them until you have a picture of where a person ought to be. Then the voice speaks from farther away. Not an echo. A whole sentence, with a breath at its beginning.
          <br><br>
          You take the bowl before deciding what that means. It is nested inside another bowl and makes a clear little ring when you lift it free. The voices stop. You keep your eyes on the shelf. Someone asks whether you need help. You say no, because you have the bowl now and because you do not want anybody standing below you while you climb down.
          <br><br>
          At the back of the shelf is an old jar with no label. You do not take it. You have reached for a bowl, not a clue, and you are tired of every ordinary thing trying to become important. You lower the bowl to the counter before stepping down. Your feet find the stool, then the floor. Both are solid. For several moments you remember how to move without treating the room like a question. <span class='blue'>The pantry gives you a different angle on the voices without keeping you close to the door.</span> You carry the bowl to the table. A parent asks why you took so long. You say the bowls were stuck together. This time the explanation is exactly the thing that happened.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你一点点挪凳子，不让它的腿擦地。比预料沉，你想要人帮，可帮忙就又有人站在身旁。你试图拥有一个没人决定该站哪里的片刻。
          <br><br>
          站上凳子，就能越过台面看。隔壁门框后露出椅子的一角，那是来客的椅子，手搁在扶手上，旁边桌上有杯子。你来回看，终于拼出一个人该在什么位置。声音却从更远处说话，不是回声，是一整句，开头带着呼吸。
          <br><br>
          你先拿碗，不先决定这意味着什么。它套在另一个碗里，提开时清清地响。大人停下说话，你盯着架子。有人问需不需要帮助。你说不用，因为已经拿到碗，也因为下凳时不想有人站在脚下。
          <br><br>
          架子深处有个没标签的旧罐，你不去拿。伸手为的是碗，不是线索，你已经厌烦每件普通东西都想变得重要。你先把碗放到台上，再下来。脚找到凳子，随后找到地板，两处都很实。几瞬间，你重新记起怎样移动，而不把房间当成问题。<span class='blue'>储藏架给了不同角度，不必一直贴在门边听。</span>你端碗到桌上，被问怎么这么久。你说，碗卡在一起。这一次，解释恰好就是发生的事。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-understood'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You bring supper to the doorway. The room beyond is not the room you saw while working. There is a long table now. Chairs stand on both sides, and one at the end. A cloth reaches almost to the floor. Beneath it, you can see shoes pointing in different directions. You cannot count the people without counting the shoes, and you do not trust the shoes.
          <br><br>
          The quieter visitor asks you to put the food down. You look for an empty place. Every place seems to belong to somebody, even the ones without plates. Your parent takes the dish from you and sets it in the middle. The little act is familiar enough to make your throat tighten. You have just been helped by someone you also heard breathing beneath a broken bed.
          <br><br>
          "Now," the visitor says. You think the conversation you were promised has finally begun. You ask for the words from the landing to be said again. Somebody repeats one, not the others. Agreement. You ask who agreed. Somebody else says willing. You want to know who was willing. A chair moves at the far end, and the movement stops your question halfway through.
          <br><br>
          The table is being set with answers instead of plates. Each person gives you one small piece and looks pleased when you take it. None says where they were when the glass broke. You realise you have not asked that yet. You have been asking what it meant, which lets anybody speak. What they saw would make them choose a place to stand. <span class='teal'>What you remember hearing is not the same as what somebody saw.</span> You step aside so you can see the doorway behind the visitor. The tablecloth stirs against a shoe that has not moved. You do not go closer to check what stirred it.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你把晚饭端到门口，里面已经不是刚才准备饭菜时看见的那间屋子。现在有一张长桌，两侧摆着椅子，尽头也有一把。桌布几乎拖到地上，下面的鞋朝着不同方向。你得数鞋才能数出人，却又不敢相信鞋能告诉你谁在哪里。
          <br><br>
          来客让你把食物放下。你寻找空处，每个位置都像属于某个人，包括那些没有盘子的。父母接过食物，放在中央。这个小动作熟悉得让喉咙发紧。刚刚帮你的，也是你听见在破床下呼吸过的人。
          <br><br>
          “现在。”来客说。你以为承诺的谈话终于开始，便请他们把楼梯口的话再说一遍。有人重复一个词，没有其余。约定。你问谁定的，另一个人说自愿。你想问谁自愿，桌子尽头却有椅子挪动，把问题打断。
          <br><br>
          桌上像在摆答案，而不是摆盘子。每人给你一小片，见你接住就高兴，却没人说玻璃碎时自己在哪里。你才发现，还没问过这件事。你一直问意味着什么，这允许任何人发言。若问亲眼看见什么，就得选一个站过的位置。<span class='teal'>你记得听过的话，不等于有人亲眼见过。</span>你侧身，看清来客身后的门。桌布擦过没移动的鞋，你却不敢凑近确认是谁让它动。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-guest-room'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          Your parents call this the room for guests. You remember it containing a smaller table and a window that looked towards the garden. There is no window now. The wall where it should be has a patch lighter than the rest, as though a picture was taken down. You stare at the patch while a voice explains that somebody should have known better.
          <br><br>
          Should have known is a large thing to put into a small room. You ask what anybody actually knew. The visitor repeats what you have heard already, only more slowly. Maybe they think saying it slowly makes it true. Your parent adjusts a plate that contains no food and tells you to listen politely.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-left-gap')>>You touch the folded page in your pocket. There is still a gap on it. The gap is more reliable than the new sentence, because you know exactly why you left it.<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-crossed-out')>>You touch the page where you crossed your own words out. You remember how quickly a complete answer made you feel safe. You look at the adults and wonder whether the same thing can happen to them.<<else>>You touch the folded paper without taking it out. It has two sides, and you can remember what is written on only one at a time.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          At the far end of the room is another chair. It faces the wall instead of the table. Nobody tells you who belongs there. The visitor says you may ask your question now. You have already asked several, and none has been answered in the way you meant. You can ask for a person who was actually present. You can ask which event happened first. Either would disturb the neat arrangement. The chair facing the wall rocks once, very slightly. You keep your own chair between you and it. You do not want a demonstration before you know what is supposed to be demonstrated.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          父母说，这是待客的屋子。你记得里面原本有较小的桌，窗户朝花园。现在没有窗了。该有窗的墙上，一块比其余浅，像刚取下一幅画。你看着浅处，听声音解释某个人本该更明白。
          <br><br>
          本该明白，是放进小屋里很大的话。你问，谁实际知道什么。来客把听过的话重复一遍，只说得更慢。也许他们以为，说慢一点，事情就变成真的了。父母摆正没有食物的盘子，叫你有礼貌地听。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-left-gap')>>你碰着口袋里的纸，空缺还在。它比新句子可靠，因为你知道自己为何留它。<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-crossed-out')>>你碰到划掉字的位置，记得完整答案多快就让人安心。你看大人，怀疑他们会不会也如此。<<else>>你碰着折纸，没有拿出来。它有两面，你一次只能记住一面写了什么。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          房间尽头还有一把椅子，朝着墙，背对餐桌。没有人说属于谁。来客说，现在可以问了。你已经问过几个问题，没有一个按原本的意思得到回答。可以问当时谁在场，也可以问哪件事先发生。两样都会弄乱整齐的摆法。面墙的椅子轻轻摇一下。你把自己的椅子挡在中间。在知道要演示什么以前，你不想要任何演示。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-name'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-asked-order')>>You ask what happened first. One adult begins with an agreement. Another begins with a visit. The visitor begins with a punishment. The beginnings do not match, but the adults speak over one another so that you cannot compare them aloud.<<else>>You ask who was in the house that night. Your parent says you were. That is not what you meant. The visitor says somebody came. No name follows. You wait so long that the person looking at you begins to seem annoyed by the waiting.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.known.bishop>>A name you heard from the bishop almost comes out of your mouth. You hold it back. You heard it somewhere else, in another room, at another age. Giving it to the visitor now would let the dream pretend somebody here had said it first.<<else>>You think there must be a name that would make everything fit. You look at each adult in turn. The longer you look, the less certain you become that the faces belong to the voices answering.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          The chair facing the wall turns. Not all at once. One front leg lifts, sets down farther round, then the other follows. Nobody takes hold of the back. You see a foot beneath it and stop thinking about the name. The foot is bare. The person sitting cannot have moved the front legs with it in that direction. The tablecloth begins sliding towards the chair, though the cloth is still weighted by plates.
          <br><br>
          Your parent tells you to keep sitting. The words reach you in the voice used for ordinary table manners. A silver nail appears beneath the turned chair, catching a fold of cloth. Another follows. The hand pulls, and a plate falls from the table before anybody reaches to stop it. It breaks near your shoe. You draw your feet back. <span class='red'>The chair is moving towards you. Remaining seated will not keep the space beneath the table clear.</span> For the first time since the visitor began explaining, the adults have stopped looking pleased. They are looking at your chair, not at what is crawling out behind it.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-asked-order')>>你问什么先发生。一个大人从约定开始，另一个从来访开始，来客从惩罚开始。开头对不上，他们又彼此盖过，让你没机会把差别说出来。<<else>>你问那一晚谁在家里。父母说，你在。你不是问这个。来客说，有人来过，却没有名字。你等得太久，看你的人开始被等候惹烦。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.known.bishop>>你差点说出从主教那里听过的名字，随后忍住。那是在另一间屋、另一个年龄听见的。此刻给来客，梦就能假装是这里的人先说。<<else>>你以为总有个能让所有事对上的名字，便逐个看大人。看得越久，越不确定脸属于回答的声音。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          面墙的椅子转了，不是一下。它抬起一条前腿，换个位置落下，另一条跟着，没人扶椅背。你看见下面一只光脚，立刻不再想名字。坐着的人不可能用那只脚把前腿朝那个方向挪。桌布开始滑向椅子，明明上面还压着盘子。
          <br><br>
          父母叫你坐好，是平常纠正餐桌规矩的声音。转过的椅子下伸出银指甲，勾住布，又一根跟上。手往后拉，谁也没拦，一只盘子落在鞋旁碎了。你缩回脚。<span class='red'>椅子正在靠近。继续坐着，桌下那片空处也不会干净。</span>自从来客开始解释，大人第一次不再高兴。他们却只看你的椅子，不看后面正爬出来的东西。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-door'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You stand. The table is too close for the chair to move back easily. You turn sideways, trying not to put your hand where the broken plate has fallen. The chair facing the wall stops turning. Something beneath it continues moving after the chair has stopped, making the wood knock against the floor in little impatient blows.
          <br><br>
          There is a door behind the visitor. It was not visible when the visitor sat down. It is visible now because everyone has shifted to look at you. You want to reach it. You also want to see whose hand has taken the cloth. The two wishes pull your eyes in opposite directions until the room seems to move without you moving at all.
          <br><br>
          A parent says your name, sharply. You look at the face. The mouth is closed. From beneath the chair comes your name again, scraped into something almost like a word. You recognise enough of it to stop. The reaching hand has a blue mark near the nail. You cannot be sure it is the mark beside your plate earlier. The rest of the hand is longer, and there is no reason a small blue mark should be proof of anything.
          <br><br>
          The visitor says you will understand if you watch. You have been told that all afternoon. Understanding has become a reason to leave you standing beside danger. You can try to remain long enough to see what the hands actually do, or your fear may drive you through the door before that is possible. <span class='blue'>Low enough fear lets you watch while keeping a way out. If you flee, you can still regain your footing beyond the door.</span> One of the silver nails taps the floor. The tap is followed by another beneath your own chair, much nearer than it should be.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你站起身。桌子太近，椅子不容易拉开，只能侧着挤，尽量不把手放到碎盘子落下的地方。面墙的椅子不再转，底下的东西却继续动，让木头一下下敲地，细碎而不耐烦。
          <br><br>
          来客背后有扇门，刚坐下时看不见。现在看见，因为所有人都挪着看你。你想过去，也想看清是谁的手拉走桌布。两个愿望把眼睛扯向不同方向，让屋子像自行移动，自己却没动。
          <br><br>
          父母很急地叫名字。你看那张脸，嘴是闭着的。椅子底下又传来名字，刮擦得几乎像个词。你认出够多，停住。伸来的手在指甲边有蓝痕，不确定是不是刚才盘边那一点。其余部分太长，小蓝痕也不该证明一切。
          <br><br>
          来客说，看了就会明白。整个下午都在这样说，明白却变成把你留在危险旁的理由。你可以试着多留一点，看手实际做什么。也可能在做得到以前，就被不安赶出门。<span class='blue'>不安够低，才可以边看边留退路。逃出去，也能在门后重新站稳。</span>一根银指甲敲地，随后自己的椅子下也敲了一下，比该有的近得多。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-stayed'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-unfinished')>>You left the landing before the sentence was finished. The missing words are still following you.<<else>>You keep one hand against the back of your chair and use it to feel how far you can move.<</if>> Looking is not the same as moving closer. You repeat that to yourself while the hand beneath the other chair opens. There is another shape behind it, too far under the cloth to see clearly. You cannot tell whether it is a second person or the first folded into a place no person should fit.
          <br><br>
          A face turns towards the light. Pale eyes, a hollow cheek, a mouth opening without making the word you are waiting for. You know whose face you want it to be. You also know that wanting will not put the familiar parts back. The fingers close on the cloth and drag it farther. Your parent at the table says nothing. You look between that face and the one near the floor until neither seems able to prove the other false.
          <br><br>
          The reaching hand does not take the food. It moves towards your pocket. You press the folded page against your leg before thinking. The movement brings the eyes towards you. You are afraid that protecting the paper has made it look more valuable than it is. It is only a shopping list and some words you did not hear properly. You have made it into something to be taken.
          <br><br>
          You move along the room's edge. The hand follows until a chair leg blocks the arm. There is a little jerk, then a scraping breath. That is your moment. <span class='green'>You reach the doorway without stepping over the body.</span> You could pull the door almost shut from this side, leaving the reaching fingers behind it, or keep watching the opening until you know it is not beside your ankles. <span class='blue'>You saw the hands' reach. You can use the door to limit it without going back towards them.</span> Behind you, the visitor resumes the explanation. It begins in exactly the place where the first plate fell, as though nothing important has interrupted it.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-unfinished')>>那句话还没说完，你就离开了楼梯口。漏掉的词还在跟着你。<<else>>你一手按着椅背，靠它感觉还能挪多远。<</if>>看，不是靠近。你反复告诉自己，直到另一把椅子下面的手展开。后面还有一个形状，藏在布下太深，看不清。你分不出是第二个人，还是第一个人折进了人不该装得下的空处。
          <br><br>
          一张脸转向光。苍白的眼睛，陷下的脸颊，张开的嘴没有说出你等的那个词。你知道希望是谁，也知道愿望不能把熟悉的部分放回去。手指攥住布，又拖了一下。桌边的父母不说话。你在地面上那张脸与这张脸之间来回看，谁都无法证明另一张是假的。
          <br><br>
          手不拿食物，朝你的口袋来。还没想，你已经把折纸压在腿上，动作引来目光。你害怕保护它，反而让它显得比实际贵重。只有购物单，几句没听好的话，却变成能被抢的东西。
          <br><br>
          你沿屋边挪，手跟来。椅腿挡住胳膊，猛地一顿，传来刮擦的呼吸。这是机会。<span class='green'>你不用跨过身体就到了门口</span>，可以从这边把门推得几乎关上，将手指留在后面。也可以盯着空处，确认不在脚踝旁。<span class='blue'>你看清了手能伸多远，可以借门挡住，不必再靠过去。</span>背后的来客继续解释，恰好接在第一只盘子掉下的位置，好像没被任何重要的事打断。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-bolted'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          You get out between the chair and the wall. There is a sharp pull at your shirt. You do not look down to see whether the cloth caught on wood or fingers. Looking would mean stopping. You reach the door, turn through it, and strike your shoulder against the frame hard enough to make your eyes water.
          <br><br>
          For a moment the passage is full of the sound you have been trying not to make. Your breath, your shoes, a small noise that might be a sob. You put a hand against the wall. The plaster is cool. You press until you can feel which fingers are touching it. Five. You count them twice. Nothing is holding the other hand, though you keep trying to pull it free.
          <br><br>
          From the room comes the visitor's calm voice. "That is why." You almost go back. Why what? Your shoulder is throbbing where it struck the frame. You press your lips together, keeping the question in. Behind the door, the same voice starts explaining again.
          <br><br>
          You keep your palm on the wall until your breathing slows. Your shoulder hurts whenever you lift that arm. You lower it, turn towards the corner, and count the boards beneath your feet. <span class='blue'>Fleeing did not end the dream. You can catch your breath here and continue along the passage.</span> Behind you, a chair knocks once against the door. You do not wait for a second knock before moving.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你挤过椅子与墙，衣服被猛地扯了一下。你不低头确认挂了木头还是手指，看就得停。到门口转出去，肩膀撞在门框上，撞得眼里生水。
          <br><br>
          一瞬间，过道塞满了你刚才拼命不想发出的声音。喘气，鞋响，一小声也许是抽泣。你扶住墙，灰泥很凉。一直按到分清哪几根手指在碰，五根，数了两次。另一只手根本没有被握住，你却还总想挣开。
          <br><br>
          屋里，来客平静地说：“就是这个原因。”你差点回去。所以什么？撞过门框的肩膀一跳跳地痛。你抿紧嘴，把问题咽回去。门后，同一个声音又开始解释。
          <br><br>
          你把手按在墙上，等呼吸慢下来。一抬胳膊，肩膀就痛。你放下手，转向拐角，数着脚下的木板。<span class='blue'>逃走没有结束梦。你可以在这里缓口气，继续沿过道走。</span>背后，一只椅子撞在门上，只一下。你没等第二下，就继续往前。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-corridor'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-shut-reaching-door')>>You pull the door almost closed. It meets something soft before the latch, and you let go at once. You have not gone back to look. A little gap remains, too narrow for the hand to come through at its earlier angle. You move while the gap is still narrow.<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-counted-floor')>>You count the boards beside the wall. The counting does not make you brave, but it tells you when you have actually taken a step instead of imagining one. Four, five, six. You stop listening for the visitor long enough to reach the corner.<<else>>You keep to the wall and watch the opening behind you without walking backwards. The room cannot follow if you keep seeing where its door is. You do not know whether that rule is true. It gives you a way to begin moving.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-fled-chair')>>Your shoulder still hurts where you hit the frame. It is a useful kind of hurt: it tells you which way you came from.<<else>>At the corner, the passage has changed.<</if>> You should be able to see the stairs. There is a second door instead, with the same brass plate. Light shows beneath it. You look behind you. The first door is still there. You have not walked in a circle. Somebody in the second room laughs the short laugh from the landing, and you feel the unfinished sentence beginning in your head again.
          <br><br>
          The floor slopes towards the second door. You put your heel across a seam in the boards to stop sliding. Your shoe holds. That little success matters more than deciding whose voice is behind the wood. You move one hand along the wall and find an old nail where a picture has been taken down. The nail catches your sleeve. You lift the cloth free with the other hand rather than pulling.
          <br><br>
          Ahead, a pale rectangle on the wall marks another absent thing. Behind you, something breathes close to the bottom of a door. You stop treating every mark as a clue and begin treating them as places to put your hands. The folded page presses against your leg. You can use it to keep track of what has changed, but it cannot tell you which voice deserves to be followed. The next corner is visible. You make for that instead of any voice that says your name.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-shut-reaching-door')>>你把门推得几乎关上，在门闩前抵到软东西，立刻松手，不回去看。只剩一条窄缝，手不能按刚才的角度伸出来。你趁缝仍窄，赶紧移动。<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-counted-floor')>>你数着墙边木板。数数没有让人勇敢，却能确认真的迈了一步，不是只想了一步。四，五，六。你暂时不听来客，终于走到转角。<<else>>你沿墙走，盯着身后空处，却不倒着走。一直看见门在哪里，房间就不能跟上。你不知道规则真假，至少给了开始移动的办法。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-fled-chair')>>撞过门框的肩膀还在痛。这种痛有用：它告诉你自己是从哪边来的。<<else>>转角后，过道变了。<</if>>本该看见楼梯，却有第二道门，一样的黄铜护板，下面透光。你回头，第一道门还在，没有走成圈。第二间里有人发出楼梯口那声短笑，你脑子里又开始那句没说完的话。
          <br><br>
          地板斜向第二道门。你把后跟横在木板接缝上，阻止滑动，鞋撑住了。这一点成功，比判断声音是谁更重要。你一手沿墙，摸到取下画后留下的旧钉，勾住袖子。用另一手挑开布，没有硬扯。
          <br><br>
          前面墙上的浅长方形，又标着一件缺少的东西。背后一扇门的底部有呼吸。你不再把每道痕当线索，开始当能放手的位置。折纸抵着腿，可以记变化，却不能决定哪个声音该跟。下一个拐角看得见。你朝那里走，不朝任何叫名字的声音走。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-map'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-seen-hands')>>You watched the reach of one hand before you left the room. That is the only direction you know is dangerous.<<else>>At the corner you take out the page.<</if>> You want to mark which doors you have passed. There is no pencil in your pocket. You use a thumbnail to crease the paper instead. One fold for the first door, another for the second. The shopping list breaks into little strips between the folds. Bread becomes two pieces of a word that could mean anything if you forgot where you tore it.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-left-gap')>>The space between your two lines remains visible. You could fold around it and keep the uncertainty in sight, even when the rest of the page has become too crowded. It would be easier to fold straight through and put everything away neatly.<<else>>The words you supplied are still beneath the crossing-out. You can put a fold through them, or leave them showing as a warning. Keeping a mistake is unpleasant. You also remember how much you wanted to keep the relief it gave you.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          You run a thumb across the first crease, then the second. Two doors. You have passed two doors. The room at the end of the passage changes while you watch it, but the creases remain beneath your thumb.
          <br><br>
          A door opens behind you. You do not turn until you have put the page against your chest. It is the laboratory door. The voices have stopped. You can see a chair inside, and something metallic rings once. You step back against the opposite wall and check the page. The gap is still there, beside the folds.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-seen-hands')>>离开那间屋子以前，你看清了一只手能够到多远。那是你唯一确知危险的方向。<<else>>到了拐角，你拿出纸，<</if>>想标记经过哪些门，口袋却没有笔。只好用指甲折，一折是第一道，再一折是第二道。购物单被折线分成小条，“面包”成了两截。忘了从哪儿撕开，拼起来就像另外一个词。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-left-gap')>>两道线之间的空处还在。你可以绕着折，其他字挤满时，也保留看得见的不确定。直接穿过去，整齐收好却更容易。<<else>>自己补的字仍在划线下面。你可以折过，也可以露着，作为提醒。留着错很难受，你也记得，多想保留它给过的安心。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          你用拇指摸过第一道折痕，又摸第二道。两扇门，自己已经走过两扇门。过道末端的房间在眼前改变，折痕却还在拇指下面。
          <br><br>
          背后有门打开。你先把纸压在胸口，才转头。是实验室，声音停了，里面有把椅子，金属轻响了一下。你退到对面的墙边，检查手里的纸。空缺仍在，就在折痕旁。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-laboratory'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The laboratory smells sharp enough to make you cough before entering. <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('work')>>You asked once what your parents did in here, and were told it could wait until after supper. Nobody ever finished the answer.<</if>> Shelves hold jars, tools and ordinary containers without their ordinary labels. You can see a wet patch beneath one jar. It is not moving. You watch long enough to make sure, then become angry that you needed to. A room for working should contain things that stay where people put them.
          <br><br>
          There is a coat on the chair. You recognise its shoulder, then the cuff. That means the visitor must have taken it off. You look for the person who could have done so. Beyond the chair, a strip of cloth hangs from the edge of the workbench. The cloth is torn in the same way as the rags around the body under the bed. You cannot see what it is attached to. The smell has made your eyes water, and you would like to blame that for not seeing clearly.
          <br><br>
          A hand touches the underside of the bench. Silver catches the light. You move your front foot back across the threshold before the hand has moved again. The jar remains still. The coat remains draped on the chair. The dangerous thing is not the object making a strange sound. It is the body low enough to be hidden by ordinary furniture.
          <br><br>
          From somewhere above you, a calm voice says that the work had consequences. You refuse to look away from the hand to find the speaker. The sentence could fit almost any broken thing in the house. It does not explain why the body is reaching towards your shoe now. <span class='red'>Do not step inside the hand's reach to hear the rest.</span> You can watch from the threshold or make the opening narrower with the door. Either lets you remain on the passage side. A fingertip follows the shadow your shoe leaves on the floor, stopping just before the light. You cannot tell whether it avoids the light or is only deciding how to move next.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          实验室的味道很冲，还没进去，你就咳嗽。<<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('work')>>你问过父母在这里做什么，答案被留到晚饭以后，却一直没人说完。<</if>>架上有罐子、工具、普通的容器，没有普通的标签。一只罐下湿了一块，没有动。你看够久才确认，又生气自己竟需要确认。工作的房间，东西该留在人放下的位置。
          <br><br>
          椅子上搭着外套，你先认出肩，再认出袖口。意味着来客脱过它。你寻找能做到的人。椅子后，工作台边垂着一条布，撕得与床下身体周围的破布相似，看不见连着什么。味道使眼睛流泪，你希望看不清只是因为这个。
          <br><br>
          一只手摸着工作台底下，银色接住了光。它还没再动，你先把前脚退过门槛。罐没有动，外套仍然搭着。危险的不是发怪响的道具，是藏得够低，能被普通家具挡住的身体。
          <br><br>
          上方某处，平静的声音说，工作总有后果。你不肯为了找说话的人，就移开盯手的目光。那句子套得进家里几乎所有坏东西，却不解释身体为何现在朝鞋伸。<span class='red'>不要为了听完，就走进那只手够得到的地方。</span>你能留在门槛看，也能借门让缝变窄，两样都在过道这边。指尖追着鞋留在地上的影子，停在光前。你不知道它在躲光，还是只决定怎样继续动。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-light'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-lab-slit')>>You push the door until only a strip of the bench remains visible. The reaching fingers do not come through. The voice above you grows louder, as though volume can make up for the view you have denied it. You stay on your side of the door.<<else>>You remain on the threshold and shift so the frame blocks part of your leg. The hand keeps trying to find your shadow. When you move the foot, it follows the old place for a moment before turning. You have learnt a little about its movement, not about the night that made it this way.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          The light in the room flickers. You do not see a cause. In one flicker there are two figures beneath the workbench. In the next, only a pile of cloth. You will not lean down to count them. You have already seen enough to know that getting close would be dangerous. The number cannot make the distance safer.
          <br><br>
          The calm voice asks whether you have understood. You say no. It sounds like a bad answer in a room full of adults. The voice asks what more you want. You try to put the question into words without giving it the name you half remember, without deciding whether a stranger caused it, without saying that your parents deserved anything. You want to know what actually happened. The wanting is so large that it leaves your mouth as a little unfinished sound.
          <br><br>
          At once the lights steady. The reaching hand stops. A chair is pulled out somewhere you cannot see. The house has heard that you want a complete answer and is arranging a place to give one. For a moment that feels like help. Then the chemical smell disappears too quickly. You can still see wet jars and torn cloth. A room does not lose its smell simply because somebody has begun a different conversation. You keep your hand on the doorframe until the calm voice invites you into another room. This time you will take a place for somebody who saw the night, not merely somebody willing to explain it.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-lab-slit')>>你推门，只留工作台一条，手指没有伸出来。上方的声音更大，像音量能补回被你拒绝的视野。你留在自己的这一边。<<else>>你站在门槛，挪到门框挡住一部分腿。手还在找鞋的影子，脚挪开，它先追旧位置，再转。你知道了一点动作，不是把它变成这样的那个夜晚。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          灯闪起来，你看不见原因。一次闪光里，台下有两个身影，下一次却只有布堆。你不弯下去数。已经看得够多，知道靠近危险。数量不能让距离安全。
          <br><br>
          平静的声音问，明白没有。你说没有，在满是大人的地方，像一个坏答案。声音又问还想要什么。你尽量不把半记得的名字塞进去，不先决定是不是陌生人造成，也不说父母配受什么。只想知道实际发生的事。愿望太大，出了嘴，却成一个没说完的小声。
          <br><br>
          灯立刻稳了，伸来的手停住，看不见处拉出椅子。房子听见你要完整答案，正在安排位置。一瞬间像帮助，随后化学味消失得太快。你仍看得见湿罐与破布。房间不会只因开始另一场谈话，就失掉气味。你一直按着门框，直到平静的声音邀请去另一屋。这次得为见过那一晚的人留位置，不是仅愿意解释的人。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-answer'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          Three chairs stand round the smaller table. A candle burns beside each place, although only the visitor is sitting down. You take the chair nearest the door. The visitor does not object. The third chair has been tucked in so tightly that its arms grip the tablecloth.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.known.bishop>>"They made friends with the servants of Auriga." You have heard the bishop say those words. Here they come from the visitor's hidden face. You look down at your hands, small enough to fit inside one another. They were not this small when you heard him. The candle spits, and the visitor carries on before you can ask how the words got here.<<else>>"They didn't know who they had invited in." The visitor rubs a thumb across the silver at the wrist. "By the time they found out, somebody had to shut the door." You look towards the passage. The door there is still open.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          You ask if your parents will get better. The visitor turns a cup upside down. A black wet circle spreads beneath it. "We can keep what's inside from reaching anyone else." You did not ask about anyone else. You ask again, louder. Under the table, something knocks against a chair leg.
          <br><br>
          You pull out the third chair. It makes a shriek across the floor. The visitor's hand clamps over yours before you have finished. "Who are you making room for?" You cannot answer quickly enough. The candle beside the empty place flares, showing a little handprint pressed into the seat.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          小桌周围摆着三把椅子。每个座位旁都燃着蜡烛，却只坐了来客一个人。你选靠门的那把，对方没有阻止。第三把椅子塞得很紧，扶手夹住了桌布。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.known.bishop>>“他们结交了奥里加的仆从。”你听过主教说这句话。现在，它从来客被遮住的脸那边传出。你低头看手，小得一只手能包住另一只。听见主教那次，它们没这么小。蜡烛爆了一点火星，来客已经继续往下说，不给你问这句话怎么进来的机会。<<else>>“他们不知道，请进来的究竟是谁。”来客的拇指摩挲腕边的银色。“等发现的时候，就得有人把门关上。”你望向过道。那里的门还开着。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          你问，父母会好吗？来客把杯子倒扣，下面扩出一道黑色的湿圈。“能让里面的东西不再伤到其他人。”你没问其他人。你又问一次，更响。桌下，有东西撞上椅腿。
          <br><br>
          你拉出第三把椅子。椅脚刮得刺耳，还没拉完，来客的手已经压住你的。“你在给谁留位置？”你没能立刻答上来。空座位旁的烛火猛地蹿高，照出椅面上一个小小的手印。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-threshold'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The visitor lets go. The fingers leave five pale places on your hand. You rub them with your other thumb. There is no pain yet. The empty chair has a cup in front of it now. You did not see anyone bring it.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-preserved-gap')>>You put your folded page beside the cup. The visitor runs a finger between the two little lines around your gap. "Still room for something to get through." You move the paper away.<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-kept-contradiction')>>You lay the crossed-out sentence beside the cup. The visitor smooths it with one hand. The crossed-out words show through darker than the rest. You pull it back before that hand reaches the next line.<<else>>The paper is stiff against your leg. You keep a hand over the pocket while the visitor reaches for the empty cup.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.known.auriga>>"Children of Auriga gnaw at the locks." You have heard that name elsewhere, in a voice that does not belong to this afternoon.<<else>>"They gnaw at the locks."<</if>> The words make you picture teeth round the little brass key downstairs. "We close the holes." You ask which side of the door your parents are on. The visitor lifts a candle. Its flame bends towards you even though you cannot feel a draught.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-witness-place')>>You made room for somebody who was actually there. That is why the empty chair is being watched as closely as you are.<<else>>Glass breaks behind the wall. You jump.<</if>> The visitor steadies the candle without looking round. A child cries from upstairs. You start to get up, and a hand beneath the table catches your shoe. The visitor says, "Stay where it's safe." The flame still points at your chest.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          来客松了手。五根手指在你的手背上留下浅浅的印，你用另一只拇指去揉，还不痛。空椅子前面多了只杯子，你没看见谁端过来。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-preserved-gap')>>你把折纸放到杯子旁。来客的指尖划过空缺两旁的小线。“还有能钻过去的地方。”你把纸移开。<<elseif $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-kept-contradiction')>>你把划掉的句子放在杯子旁。来客伸手抚平，那些被划掉的词比其余字迹更黑了。手伸向下一行前，你把纸抽回来。<<else>>纸硬硬地抵着腿。来客去拿空杯，你用一只手盖住口袋。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.known.auriga>>“奥里加之子在啃咬锁。”你在别处听过这个名字，说话的人不属于这个下午。<<else>>“他们在啃咬锁。”<</if>>你想到楼下那把小黄铜钥匙，周围围着一圈牙。“我们把洞补上。”你问，父母在门的哪一边？来客举起蜡烛。你感觉不到风，火焰却朝你弯过来。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-witness-place')>>你给真正在场的人留了位置。所以那把空椅子现在和你一样受人注视。<<else>>墙后有玻璃碎了，你吓得一缩。<</if>>来客扶稳蜡烛，没有回头。楼上，一个孩子哭起来。你刚要站，桌下的手就抓住了鞋。来客说：“待在安全的地方。”火焰仍指着你的胸口。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-hear-again'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The chair tips as you pull away. You catch its back and kick the table leg. The fingers let go. A wet line crosses your shoe from toe to heel. You move beyond the hanging cloth, where you can see both your feet.
          <br><br>
          Upstairs, the child calls again. It is your voice. You press your lips together and hear it a third time. The visitor puts down the candle. "That will stop if you let us finish." You ask whether someone will go up. The visitor looks at your empty chair instead.
          <br><br>
          You can make them repeat the words until they all say the same thing. You can keep listening to the child, too. The calls come between the little falls of glass. Each time, the pause before the next call is longer. You count with your fingers and lose your place when something knocks under the table.
          <br><br>
          The visitor slides the empty cup towards you. <span class='red'>The hand beneath the table can still reach your chair.</span> You stay on your feet. From upstairs comes a short, sharp sound, as though somebody has covered the child's mouth. Your parent begins speaking very quickly. The visitor waits.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          你往后挣，椅子斜了。你抓住椅背，用脚踢向桌腿，那些手指松开了。一道湿痕从鞋头横到鞋跟。你绕开垂着的桌布，退到能看见两只脚的地方。
          <br><br>
          楼上的孩子又喊，是你的声音。你抿紧嘴，第三声仍传下来。来客放下蜡烛。“让我们说完，就会停。”你问，有人上去吗？来客却看向你的空椅子。
          <br><br>
          可以叫他们再说，直到每个人都说一样的话。也能继续听孩子。呼叫夹在碎玻璃的落响之间。每一声过后，到下一声的间隔都更长。你掰着指头数，桌下一撞，就忘了数到哪里。
          <br><br>
          来客把空杯推过来。<span class='red'>桌下的手仍然够得到你的椅子。</span>你没有坐。楼上传来短促的一声，像有人捂住了孩子的嘴。父母飞快地开口，来客等着。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-table-again'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The table grows longer. Your parents sit on either side, with the visitor at the far end. There is a cup before every chair. Not one has water in it. The child's handprint slides away from you as the table lengthens, until it is almost hidden behind the visitor.
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-listened-child')>>You keep looking at the wall where the stairs should be. A little door has appeared there. When the child calls, its handle trembles.<<else>>You ask them to finish. All three begin at once. You cover one ear, then the other. From either side, you hear the visitor's voice.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          "What is said here stays here." The visitor sets a silver key beside your plate. "No names through the window. No questions at the temple. Promise, and tonight the doors will stay shut." Your parent opens their mouth, then closes it. You wish they would tell you whether to say yes.
          <br><br>
          You reach towards the key. The small door bangs once, and you draw your hand back. A voice on its other side calls for you. The visitor says your name too, very softly. You want the glass to stop breaking. You want your parents to look at you. <span class='red'>The visitor is asking you to promise that you will not tell anyone outside.</span> The key is close enough to take. The little door is still close enough to reach.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          桌子变长了。父母坐在两侧，来客坐到尽头。每个座位前都有杯子，一只也没装水。孩子的手印跟着桌子向远处滑，直到快被来客挡住。
          <br><br>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-listened-child')>>你始终盯着本该有楼梯的墙。那里出现一扇小门。孩子一喊，把手就颤一下。<<else>>你要他们说完，三个声音一齐开口。你先捂一只耳朵，又捂另一只。从哪边听，都是来客的声音。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          “在这里说的话，就留在这里。”来客把一把银钥匙放在盘子旁。“不往窗外喊名字，不到神殿问。答应了，今晚这些门就会关着。”父母张了张嘴，又闭上。你希望他们说，要不要答应。
          <br><br>
          你朝钥匙伸手。小门突然撞了一下，你又缩回来。门那边有声音喊你，来客也轻轻喊你的名字。你想让玻璃别再碎，想让父母看看你。<span class='red'>来客要你答应，不向屋外的人说。</span>钥匙仍然拿得到。那扇小门也仍来得及跑过去。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-final-question'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          "No." It comes out too quietly. You try again, and this time the visitor stops turning the cup. Your parent grips the tablecloth. The silver key slips over the edge of the plate and lands on the floor with no sound.
          <br><br>
          You ask who will go upstairs. The visitor says that you have already been spared. <<if $LostLamb.known.bishop>>For a moment there is a bloodstained altar beyond the chair, and your parents are begging someone you cannot see. You blink hard. The dining room comes back. You have heard about an altar. You have never seen this one.<<else>>Your parent puts both hands over their face. "Leave the child," they say. The visitor does not look at them.<</if>>
          <br><br>
          "If you open that door, we cannot keep you safe." The visitor speaks more loudly now. The flame bows low across the table, nearly touching your folded paper. You pull it out of your pocket, clutch it to your chest, and back towards the little door.
          <br><br>
          The child's voice comes through the wood. You can hear the breaths between the words. Behind you, the cups fall one after another. None breaks. They keep falling as you put your hand on the handle. You wish an adult would get up. Nobody does.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          “不。”太轻了。你又说一次，这次来客不再转杯子。父母攥紧桌布。银钥匙滑过盘沿，落到地上，没有声音。
          <br><br>
          你问，谁会上楼？来客说，你已经被放过一次了。<<if $LostLamb.known.bishop>>椅子后面忽然有座沾血的祭坛，父母正在求你看不见的人。你用力眨眼，餐厅回来了。你听人说过祭坛，却从没见过这座。<<else>>父母用两只手盖住脸。“放过孩子。”他们说。来客没有看他们。<</if>>
          <br><br>
          “开了那扇门，我们就保护不了你。”来客的声音更响了。火焰伏低在桌面上，快碰到你的折纸。你把纸从口袋里抽出来，抱在胸前，倒退着靠近小门。
          <br><br>
          孩子的声音穿过门板，你听得到每个词之间的喘息。身后，杯子一只接一只落下，没有一只碎。你握住门把时，它们仍在落。你希望有个大人站起来，谁也没有。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict-child-call'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          The little door opens onto the stairs. You recognise the broken edge that catches your shoe. At the top is your bedroom door. The child cries behind it, close enough that you knock before thinking what to say.
          <br><br>
          "I'm here." The answer comes from inside, at exactly the same time as you say it. You knock again. So does someone on the other side. You put your palm flat against the wood, trying to feel a hand against yours. There is only heat.
          <br><br>
          Downstairs, the visitor calls you back. The voice sounds like your parent now. It says the door will not open again if you shut it. You look over your shoulder. The little door below has a silver key already in its lock.
          <br><br>
          You push your bedroom door. The handle does not move at first. You use both hands, then your shoulder, with the folded paper crushed between you and the wood. Something tears in the pocket. The handle turns. Below, every cup stops falling at once. You stumble into the room before the voice can tell you to wait.
        <<option 'CN'>>
          小门通向楼梯。你认得会绊住鞋的破边。上面是自己的卧室门。孩子在门后哭，离得那么近，还没想好说什么，你就敲了。
          <br><br>
          “我在。”你开口的同时，屋里也说出同一句话。你又敲一次，另一边也有人敲。你把掌心平平地按上门板，想感觉到另一只手，只有热。
          <br><br>
          楼下来客喊你回去，现在听着像父母。它说，门一关，就不会再开。你回头，下面的小门已经插着一把银钥匙。
          <br><br>
          你推卧室门，把手一开始不动。先用两只手，再拿肩膀顶，折纸夹在身体与门之间。口袋里有东西撕裂。把手转了，楼下每一只杯子同时停止落下。那声音还没来得及叫你再等等，你已经撞进屋里。
      <</language>>
    <<case 'verdict'>>
      <<language>>
        <<option 'EN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-vow-sealed')>>You say that you promise. The visitor takes the key away before you can touch it. Your parent wipes the table with the wet cloth. The plates are put away. They do not ask you to help.
          <br><br>
          The little door closes. You hear the bolt pushed across from the other side. The crying stops halfway through your name. So does the glass. The sudden quiet makes your ears hurt. You wait for somebody to say that the child has been brought downstairs. The visitor stands up instead.
          <br><br>
          "Say it once more." You repeat the words. The visitor's sleeve brushes your cheek on the way past, cold and damp. Your parents remain seated while the hall door opens and shuts. No footsteps pass through it. When you turn back, an empty cup stands at your place.
          <br><br>
          You have the quiet you asked for. You put both hands round the cup and try to remember what you must never say. Your name comes first. Sydney's comes next. You squeeze harder, frightened that thinking them counts as saying them. Upstairs, something taps against the locked door, once, and then waits.<<else>>The bed is empty. You snatch back the covers and find a warm hollow in the mattress, just big enough for you. The child cries again. You put a hand over your mouth. It stops. When you move the hand away, you have to hold your breath to keep it from starting.
          <br><br>
          The door below slams shut. You run to the window instead. The catch cuts into your finger, and the first push does nothing. You try with both hands. Cold air reaches your face. There is a light in the house across the road.
          <br><br>
          You try to call to it. Your voice comes out hoarse and small. You cannot tell whether somebody has heard. The visitor calls from the stairs, using your parent's voice, and you shout again before it reaches your door. There is no answer yet.
          <br><br>
          You keep the window open with your sore hand. Your paper has torn across the folds. Half the words are on one piece, half on the other, and you cannot find the bit with your name. You hold the pieces so they will not blow away. Behind you, the door handle begins to turn. You press your chest against the sill and try once more.<</if>>
        <<option 'CN'>>
          <<if $LostLamb.marks.includes('verdict-vow-sealed')>>你说，答应了。还没碰到，来客就拿走了钥匙。父母用湿布擦桌子，收起盘子，没有叫你帮忙。
          <br><br>
          小门关上了。另一边有门闩推过去的声音。哭声刚喊到你名字的一半，便停住了，玻璃也不再碎。突然安静，震得耳朵难受。你等谁说，已经把孩子抱到楼下。来客却站起来了。
          <br><br>
          “再说一次。”你又说一遍。来客经过时，袖子擦过你的脸，冰冷而潮湿。父母仍坐着。大厅的门开了，又关，没有脚步走过。你回头，自己的座位前摆着空杯。
          <br><br>
          你得到了要的安静。两只手抱住杯子，努力记哪些词绝不能说。先是你的名字，再是悉尼的。你越抱越紧，怕想起来也算说出来。楼上，有东西在锁着的门上敲了一下，随后等着。<<else>>床里空着。你猛地掀开被子，床垫还留着温热的小凹，刚好能放下你。孩子又哭起来。你捂住嘴，声音就停。手移开，得屏住气，才不会继续。
          <br><br>
          下面的门砰地关了。你跑向窗户。窗扣硌伤手指，第一下没推开。你换两只手，冷风终于扑到脸上。对面的房子里亮着灯。
          <br><br>
          你试着朝那里喊。声音又哑又小，分不清有没有人听见。来客从楼梯那边喊你，用父母的声音。你赶在它到门前又喊一次，仍没有回答。
          <br><br>
          你用疼痛的那只手撑住窗户。纸沿着折痕撕成了两半，一半字在这张，另一半在那张，找不到写名字的那一块。你攥着纸片，不让风吹走。身后，门把开始转动。你把胸口压在窗沿上，再试一次。<</if>>
      <</language>>
  <</switch>>
<</widget>>
